// Rep Max questions: Chemistry (AQA 8462), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gch-001","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which technique is used to get pure water from salt solution?","o":["Filtration","Paper chromatography","Simple distillation","Crystallisation"],"a":2,"x":"Distillation evaporates the water and condenses it, leaving the salt behind. Crystallisation keeps the salt, and filtration only removes insoluble solids."}
{"id":"gch-002","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"A chlorine atom has atomic number 17 and mass number 37. How many neutrons does it have?","o":["17","37","20","54"],"a":2,"x":"Neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 37 − 17 = 20. The atomic number is the number of protons, which is also 17."}
{"id":"gch-003","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.7","lv":1,"q":"Phosphorus has atomic number 15. What is its electronic structure?","o":["2,8,8,5","2,5,8","2,8,5","2,13"],"a":2,"x":"15 electrons fill the shells in order: 2 in the first, 8 in the second, leaving 5 in the third shell. The structure is 2,8,5."}
{"id":"gch-004","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why did Mendeleev leave gaps in his periodic table?","o":["He did not have data on the first few elements","He ordered the elements by atomic number, which left holes","The gaps were for isotopes of the elements nearby","The gaps were for elements that had not been discovered yet"],"a":3,"x":"Mendeleev switched the order of some elements to fit patterns in properties and left gaps, predicting undiscovered elements. Later discoveries matched his predictions."}
{"id":"gch-005","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Rubidium is more reactive than lithium. What is the reason?","o":["It has more electrons in its outer shell","Its outer electron is held more strongly by the nucleus","It has a higher melting point than lithium","Its outer electron is further from the nucleus"],"a":3,"x":"Down Group 1 the outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so the attraction is weaker and the electron is lost more easily."}
{"id":"gch-006","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Compared with Group 1 metals, transition metals are generally...","o":["softer, less dense and more reactive","harder, denser and more reactive","softer, denser and less reactive","harder, denser and less reactive"],"a":3,"x":"Transition metals have high melting points, high densities and are hard and strong, and they react less vigorously than Group 1. Group 1 metals are soft and very reactive."}
{"id":"gch-007","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Aluminium forms Al³⁺ ions and oxygen forms O²⁻ ions. What is the formula of aluminium oxide?","o":["Al₂O₃","AlO","Al₃O₂","Al₂O₂"],"a":0,"x":"The charges must cancel. Two Al³⁺ give +6, and three O²⁻ give −6, so the ratio of ions is 2 : 3 and the formula is Al₂O₃."}
{"id":"gch-008","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"In a chemical equation, what does the state symbol (aq) mean?","o":["Dissolved in water","In the gas state","In the solid state","As a pure liquid"],"a":0,"x":"(aq) means aqueous: the substance is dissolved in water. The other symbols are (s) solid, (l) liquid and (g) gas."}
{"id":"gch-009","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does solid sodium chloride not conduct electricity, but molten sodium chloride does?","o":["In the molten state electrons are free to move","Molten sodium chloride contains small molecules","In the molten state the ions are free to move","Solid sodium chloride has no charged particles"],"a":2,"x":"In a solid the ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice. When melted the ions can move and carry charge, so the liquid conducts."}
{"id":"gch-010","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does graphite conduct electricity?","o":["One electron per carbon atom is delocalised and can move","It contains positive ions that are free to move through it","It is a metal, so it has a sea of free electrons","Each carbon forms four bonds, leaving no free electrons"],"a":0,"x":"In graphite each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds, leaving one delocalised electron per atom. These electrons move along the layers and carry charge."}
{"id":"gch-011","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Particle A is a cube of side 5 nm and particle B is a cube of side 50 nm. How do their surface area to volume ratios compare?","o":["B's ratio is 10 times larger than A's","Their ratios are the same","A's ratio is 10 times larger than B's","A's ratio is 100 times larger than B's"],"a":2,"x":"For a cube the ratio is 6 ÷ side length. A: 6 ÷ 5 = 1.2 and B: 6 ÷ 50 = 0.12, so A's is 10 times larger. Nanoparticles have a huge ratio."}
{"id":"gch-012","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Propane burns: C₃H₈ + ___O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. What number goes in front of O₂ to balance the equation?","o":["3","5","4","10"],"a":1,"x":"The right side has 3 × 2 + 4 = 10 oxygen atoms. Each O₂ molecule has two atoms, so 10 ÷ 2 = 5 molecules of O₂ are needed."}
{"id":"gch-013","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"What is the percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃? (Ar: N = 14, H = 1, O = 16)","o":["17.5%","35.0%","28.0%","70.0%"],"a":1,"x":"Mr = 14 + 4 + 14 + 48 = 80. There are two nitrogen atoms, 28 in total. Percentage = 28 ÷ 80 × 100 = 35.0%.","w":["Used only one nitrogen atom: 14 ÷ 80, but the formula has two N.","","Used the mass 28 as if it were the percentage; you must divide by 80.","Used 28 for each N (like N₂); each N atom is 14, so 28 ÷ 80 = 35%."]}
{"id":"gch-014","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Three titration volumes were 24.10, 24.30 and 24.20 cm³. Taking the uncertainty as half the range, what are the mean and the uncertainty?","o":["24.20 ± 0.20 cm³","24.20 ± 0.10 cm³","24.30 ± 0.10 cm³","24.20 ± 0.05 cm³"],"a":1,"x":"Mean = 72.60 ÷ 3 = 24.20 cm³. The range is 0.20, and the uncertainty is half the range: ± 0.10 cm³.","w":["Used the whole range as the uncertainty; it should be half the range.","","Mean is wrong: 24.30 is the largest value, not the mean of the three.","Halved the range twice; half of 0.20 is 0.10, not 0.05."]}
{"id":"gch-015","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"5.0 g of sodium hydroxide is dissolved in water to make 250 cm³ of solution. What is the concentration in g/dm³?","o":["1.25 g/dm³","20 g/dm³","1250 g/dm³","0.02 g/dm³"],"a":1,"x":"Convert 250 cm³ to 0.25 dm³. Concentration = mass ÷ volume = 5.0 ÷ 0.25 = 20 g/dm³.","w":["Multiplied by 0.25 instead of dividing; dividing by 0.25 gives a larger number.","","Multiplied 5.0 by 250; divide by the volume in dm³ (0.25) instead.","Divided by 250 without converting to dm³, so the answer is far too small."]}
{"id":"gch-016","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.3","lv":3,"q":"Hydrogen is made by CH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂. Mr: CH₄ = 16, H₂O = 18, H₂ = 2. What is the atom economy for making hydrogen?","o":["17.6%","5.9%","33.3%","37.5%"],"a":0,"x":"Atom economy = Mr of desired product ÷ Mr of all reactants × 100 = (3 × 2) ÷ (16 + 18) × 100 = 6 ÷ 34 × 100 = 17.6%.","w":["","Used only one H₂ (2) instead of 3H₂ (6); the equation has 3 moles of H₂.","Divided by the Mr of water only; use the total Mr of both reactants.","Divided by the Mr of methane only; use the total Mr of both reactants."]}
{"id":"gch-017","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which metal will not displace copper from copper sulfate solution?","o":["Zinc","Iron","Magnesium","Silver"],"a":3,"x":"A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one. Silver is below copper in the reactivity series, so there is no reaction.","w":["Zinc is more reactive than copper, so it does displace it.","Iron is more reactive than copper, so it does displace it.","Magnesium is much more reactive than copper, so it does displace it.",""]}
{"id":"gch-018","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student makes copper sulfate crystals by adding copper oxide to warm sulfuric acid. Why is the copper oxide added in excess?","o":["To make the reaction go faster","To make sure all the acid has reacted","To make the solution acidic at the end","To make the crystals larger"],"a":1,"x":"Copper oxide is insoluble, so the extra solid is simply filtered off, leaving a neutral salt solution with no acid left. Then it is evaporated and crystallised.","w":["Speed isn't the aim; extra oxide makes sure no acid is left over.","","Excess oxide removes the acid, so the solution ends neutral, not acidic.","Crystal size depends on how slowly it crystallises, not on excess oxide."]}
{"id":"gch-019","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"In a titration, acid from a burette is added to alkali in a flask containing phenolphthalein. What shows the end-point?","o":["Colourless to pink","Blue to green","Pink to colourless","Red to orange"],"a":2,"x":"Phenolphthalein is pink in alkali and colourless in acid. When the alkali is just neutralised the pink colour disappears.","w":["This is the reverse: it fits alkali being added to acid, not acid to alkali.","Phenolphthalein is not blue or green in any solution; those aren't its colours.","","Red to orange is not a phenolphthalein change; it is pink in alkali, colourless in acid."]}
{"id":"gch-020","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite for electrolysis?","o":["To make the aluminium that forms harder","To react with the oxygen that forms at the anode","To stop the carbon anodes from burning away","To lower the melting point and save energy"],"a":3,"x":"Aluminium oxide melts at over 2000 °C. Dissolving it in molten cryolite lowers the temperature needed, which saves energy and cost.","w":["Cryolite does not affect the metal's hardness; it affects the melting temperature.","Oxygen reacts with the carbon anodes, not the cryolite.","The anodes still burn away and need replacing; cryolite doesn't prevent this.",""]}
{"id":"gch-021","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.4","lv":3,"q":"Copper sulfate solution is electrolysed with inert electrodes. What forms at each electrode?","o":["Copper at the cathode and oxygen at the anode","Hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode","Copper at the cathode and hydrogen at the anode","Hydrogen at the cathode and sulfur at the anode"],"a":0,"x":"Copper is less reactive than hydrogen, so copper forms at the cathode. Sulfate ions are not discharged, so hydroxide ions lose electrons to give oxygen at the anode.","w":["","Hydrogen would only form at the cathode if the metal were more reactive than hydrogen.","Hydrogen ions are positive, so they go to the cathode, never the anode.","Sulfur is not formed; sulfate ions stay in solution and hydroxide gives oxygen."]}
{"id":"gch-022","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":1,"q":"On a reaction profile the products are at a lower energy than the reactants. What does this show?","o":["The reaction is endothermic and takes in energy","The reaction can only happen with a catalyst","The reaction needs no activation energy","The reaction is exothermic and gives out energy"],"a":3,"x":"If the products have less energy than the reactants, the difference is transferred to the surroundings, so the temperature rises. This is exothermic.","w":["Mixed up the terms: endothermic would have products at higher energy.","Catalysts only lower the activation energy; they don't decide the energy change.","Every reaction needs activation energy to get started, even exothermic ones.",""]}
{"id":"gch-023","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the only product of a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell?","o":["Carbon dioxide","Ammonia","Hydrogen peroxide","Water"],"a":3,"x":"Hydrogen is oxidised in the cell: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. The only product is water, so no carbon dioxide is released.","w":["CO₂ comes from burning fossil fuels; this cell has no carbon in it.","There is no nitrogen in the reactants, so ammonia cannot form.","Peroxide has extra oxygen; the cell's overall reaction gives H₂O only.",""]}
{"id":"gch-024","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A gas syringe shows 24 cm³ at 20 s and 44 cm³ at 60 s. What is the mean rate between 20 s and 60 s?","o":["0.73 cm³/s","1.2 cm³/s","2.0 cm³/s","0.5 cm³/s"],"a":3,"x":"Mean rate = change in volume ÷ change in time = (44 − 24) ÷ (60 − 20) = 20 ÷ 40 = 0.5 cm³/s.","w":["Divided the final volume 44 by 60 s; you need the change in both volume and time.","Used 24 ÷ 20, the rate for the first 20 s only, not 20 s to 60 s.","Divided time by volume (40 ÷ 20) the wrong way round; rate is volume ÷ time.",""]}
{"id":"gch-025","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Why does raising the temperature make a reaction faster?","o":["Particles collide more often and with more energy","It lowers the activation energy of the reaction","There are more particles in the same volume","The particles collide less often but with more energy"],"a":0,"x":"Hotter particles move faster, so collisions are more frequent. A greater proportion also have at least the activation energy, so more collisions are successful.","w":["","Mixes this up with a catalyst; heating does not lower the activation energy.","That is concentration or pressure; heating adds no extra particles to the volume.","Hotter particles move faster, so they collide more often, not less."]}
{"id":"gch-026","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is true about a reversible reaction at dynamic equilibrium in a closed system?","o":["The forward and reverse reactions happen at the same rate","Both reactions have stopped","The concentrations of reactants and products are equal","Only the forward reaction is still happening"],"a":0,"x":"At equilibrium both reactions continue at equal rates, so the concentrations stay constant. They are not necessarily equal.","w":["","Equilibrium is dynamic: both reactions carry on, just at equal rates.","Equal rates do not mean equal concentrations; they just stay constant.","In a reversible reaction the reverse reaction carries on too."]}
{"id":"gch-027","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Compared with a fraction made of long hydrocarbon chains, a fraction of short chains has...","o":["a higher boiling point and is more flammable","a lower boiling point and is more viscous","a higher boiling point and is more viscous","a lower boiling point and is more flammable"],"a":3,"x":"Shorter chains have weaker forces between molecules, so they boil at lower temperatures, flow easily (low viscosity) and ignite more easily.","w":["Short chains have weaker forces between molecules, so a lower boiling point, not higher.","Lower boiling point is right, but short chains are less viscous, not more.","Both wrong: short chains boil at lower temperatures and flow more easily.",""]}
{"id":"gch-028","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.4","lv":1,"q":"What happens when an alkene is shaken with bromine water?","o":["The bromine water changes from colourless to orange","The bromine water changes from orange to colourless","The bromine water stays orange","The bromine water turns blue"],"a":1,"x":"Alkenes have a C=C double bond. Bromine adds across it, so the orange bromine water is decolourised. Alkanes do not react.","w":["Colours reversed: bromine water starts orange and ends colourless.","","That is what alkanes do; alkenes react with bromine and decolourise it.","Bromine water never turns blue; alkenes decolourise it."]}
{"id":"gch-029","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is made when ethene reacts with steam in the presence of a catalyst?","o":["Ethane","Ethanoic acid","Poly(ethene)","Ethanol"],"a":3,"x":"Steam adds across the double bond: C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. This is one way to make ethanol industrially.","w":["Ethane would need hydrogen added, not steam.","Ethanoic acid needs oxidation; steam just adds H and OH across the C=C.","Poly(ethene) forms when many ethene molecules join, not by reacting with steam.",""]}
{"id":"gch-030","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":2,"q":"What is the formula of propanoic acid?","o":["CH₃CH₂COOH","CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","CH₃COOH","CH₃CH₂CHO"],"a":0,"x":"Carboxylic acids contain –COOH. Propanoic acid has three carbon atoms, so it is CH₃CH₂COOH. CH₃COOH is ethanoic acid.","w":["","That is propanol (an alcohol, –OH); propanoic acid needs –COOH.","That is ethanoic acid with two carbons; propanoic acid has three.","That is propanal (an aldehyde, –CHO), not a carboxylic acid."]}
{"id":"gch-031","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which molecule can act as a monomer in addition polymerisation?","o":["CH₃CH₂Cl","CH₃CH₃","CH₂=CHCl","C₂H₅OH"],"a":2,"x":"Addition polymers form from monomers with a C=C double bond. CH₂=CHCl (chloroethene) is the monomer for poly(chloroethene).","w":["Saturated, with no C=C double bond, so it cannot add on to form a polymer.","Ethane is saturated; no double bond means no addition polymerisation.","","Ethanol has no C=C bond; it is an alcohol, not an addition monomer."]}
{"id":"gch-032","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":1,"q":"DNA is a polymer. What are its monomers called?","o":["Amino acids","Glucose units","Nucleotides","Fatty acids"],"a":2,"x":"DNA is made of nucleotides, each with one of four bases. Amino acids make proteins and glucose units make starch and cellulose.","w":["Amino acids are the monomers of proteins, not DNA.","Glucose units make starch and cellulose, not DNA.","","Fatty acids join with glycerol to make lipids, not DNA."]}
{"id":"gch-033","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A pure compound melts at 122 °C. A sample melts over the range 114–119 °C. What does this suggest?","o":["The sample is pure","The sample is a different compound with a sharper melting point","The sample is impure","The thermometer must be broken"],"a":2,"x":"Pure substances have a sharp melting point. Impurities lower the melting point and make it melt over a range of temperatures.","w":["Pure compounds melt sharply at one temperature, not over a range.","","","No need to blame the thermometer; a lower, wider range is the sign of impurities."]}
{"id":"gch-034","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":2,"q":"In chromatography a spot travels 3.2 cm and the solvent front travels 8.0 cm. What is the Rf value?","o":["2.5","0.40","0.25","4.8"],"a":1,"x":"Rf = distance moved by the spot ÷ distance moved by the solvent = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.40. It has no units and is never more than 1.","w":["Divided solvent by spot (8.0 ÷ 3.2); Rf is spot distance ÷ solvent distance.","","Slip in the division: 3.2 ÷ 8.0 is 0.40, not 0.25.","Subtracted the distances (8.0 − 3.2) instead of dividing."]}
{"id":"gch-035","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.2","lv":1,"q":"A gas bleaches damp litmus paper. Which gas is it?","o":["Hydrogen","Oxygen","Chlorine","Carbon dioxide"],"a":2,"x":"Chlorine turns damp blue litmus red and then bleaches it white. Hydrogen gives a squeaky pop and carbon dioxide turns limewater cloudy.","w":["Hydrogen gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint; it does not bleach litmus.","Oxygen relights a glowing splint; it does not bleach litmus.","","Carbon dioxide turns limewater cloudy; it does not bleach litmus."]}
{"id":"gch-036","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A white solid gives a lilac flame. It dissolves, and barium chloride with dilute hydrochloric acid gives a white precipitate. What is the solid?","o":["Potassium sulfate","Potassium chloride","Sodium sulfate","Potassium carbonate"],"a":0,"x":"A lilac flame shows K⁺. A white precipitate with barium chloride and acid shows sulfate. Sodium gives a yellow flame, and carbonates fizz with acid.","w":["","Chloride is tested with silver nitrate; the barium chloride test shows sulfate.","Sodium burns yellow, not lilac; lilac means potassium.","A carbonate would fizz with acid, and the barium test shows sulfate."]}
{"id":"gch-037","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.6","lv":1,"q":"Which is an advantage of instrumental methods over chemical tests?","o":["They are rapid, accurate and sensitive","They are always cheaper","They need no trained operator","They give a result by colour change"],"a":0,"x":"Instrumental methods are quick, accurate and can detect tiny samples. However, the equipment is expensive and needs trained operators.","w":["","Equipment is usually costly to buy and maintain, so not always cheaper.","Instruments need trained operators, so this is the opposite of true.","Colour change is a feature of simple chemical tests, not instruments."]}
{"id":"gch-038","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.1","lv":1,"q":"Which process caused the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere to increase over billions of years?","o":["Gases given out by volcanoes","Photosynthesis by algae and plants","Burning of fossil fuels by humans","Condensation of water vapour into oceans"],"a":1,"x":"Algae and later plants used carbon dioxide for photosynthesis and released oxygen, so oxygen rose and carbon dioxide fell.","w":["Volcanoes mainly released CO₂, water vapour and nitrogen, not much oxygen.","","Fossil fuel burning uses up oxygen and began only recently, not over billions of years.","Condensation formed the oceans, but it does not produce oxygen."]}
{"id":"gch-039","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.2.1","lv":2,"q":"How do greenhouse gases keep the Earth warm?","o":["They absorb infrared radiation from the Earth and re-radiate it","They reflect ultraviolet radiation away from the Earth","They trap heat by stopping the Sun's rays reaching the surface","They absorb all the light from the Sun"],"a":0,"x":"Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide and methane absorb long-wavelength infrared radiation emitted from the Earth and radiate some of it back.","w":["","Ozone deals with UV; greenhouse gases absorb infrared given out by the Earth.","Greenhouse gases let sunlight through; they trap heat leaving the Earth.","They do not absorb all sunlight; most reaches the surface."]}
{"id":"gch-040","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.3","lv":2,"q":"Why is carbon monoxide poisonous?","o":["It stops the blood carrying oxygen","It causes acid rain","It fills the lungs with soot","It reacts with carbon dioxide in the blood"],"a":0,"x":"Carbon monoxide is colourless and odourless. It combines with haemoglobin and prevents red blood cells carrying oxygen, and it forms in incomplete combustion.","w":["","Acid rain comes from sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, not carbon monoxide.","Soot is a separate product; CO is a gas that stops blood carrying oxygen.","It binds to haemoglobin; it does not react with CO₂ in the blood."]}
{"id":"gch-041","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a finite resource?","o":["Wood from sustainably managed forests","Wind","Crude oil","Sunlight"],"a":2,"x":"Finite resources are used up faster than they form. Crude oil takes millions of years to form. Wood, wind and sunlight are renewable.","w":["Sustainably managed forests are replanted, so wood is renewable.","Wind never runs out, so it is renewable.","","Sunlight is continually supplied, so it is renewable."]}
{"id":"gch-042","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Why is chlorine added when drinking water is treated?","o":["To remove insoluble solids","To kill microorganisms","To remove dissolved salts","To make the water taste better"],"a":1,"x":"Sterilising uses chlorine, ozone or ultraviolet light to kill microbes. Filtration removes solids.","w":["Insoluble solids are removed by filtration, not by adding chlorine.","","Dissolved salts are removed by distillation, not by chlorination.","Chlorine is added to sterilise the water, not to improve taste."]}
{"id":"gch-043","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.2","lv":1,"q":"What is one benefit of recycling aluminium instead of extracting it from its ore?","o":["It uses much less energy","It makes a purer metal","It uses more electricity","It gives off more carbon dioxide"],"a":0,"x":"Extracting aluminium needs lots of electricity. Recycling saves energy, conserves ore and reduces waste.","w":["","Recycled aluminium is the same metal; purity is not the main benefit.","Recycling uses less electricity, not more.","Recycling cuts CO₂ emissions because it uses less energy."]}
{"id":"gch-044","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.3","lv":2,"q":"Why is steel harder than pure iron?","o":["Steel contains more iron atoms packed into each layer","Steel has no metallic bonding between its iron atoms","The layers of atoms in steel are more regular than in iron","Carbon atoms distort the layers, so they slide less easily"],"a":3,"x":"In steel the carbon atoms are a different size, so the layers of iron atoms cannot slide over each other as easily as in pure iron.","w":["It is the added carbon atoms that make steel harder, not extra iron atoms.","Steel is still metallic; the bonding is still there.","Pure iron has the regular layers, which slide easily; steel's are less regular.",""]}
{"id":"gch-045","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.4","lv":3,"q":"The Haber process runs at about 450 °C, not lower. Why, given that the reaction is exothermic?","o":["A lower temperature gives a lower yield and a slower rate","A lower temperature makes the catalyst react with nitrogen","At 450 °C the yield of ammonia is the highest possible","Lower temperatures give more ammonia, but too slowly"],"a":3,"x":"The reaction is exothermic, so a lower temperature favours more ammonia. But the rate would be too slow, so 450 °C is a compromise between rate and yield.","w":["Lower temperature gives a higher yield for an exothermic reaction, not lower.","The catalyst is not used up by reacting with nitrogen.","Yield is actually higher at lower temperatures, so 450 °C is not the maximum.",""]}
{"id":"gch-046","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Calcium carbonate decomposes: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. What mass of carbon dioxide forms from 10.0 g of CaCO₃? (Ar: Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)","o":["0.44 g","4.4 g","44 g","10.0 g"],"a":1,"x":"Mr of CaCO₃ = 100, so 10.0 g is 0.100 mol. The ratio is 1 : 1, so 0.100 mol CO₂ forms. Mass = 0.100 × 44 = 4.4 g.","w":["Decimal slip: 0.100 × 44 is 4.4 g, not 0.44 g.","","Forgot to use 0.100 mol: 44 g is the mass of one whole mole of CO₂.","Assumed the product mass equals the reactant mass; CaO has mass too."]}
{"id":"gch-047","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.4","lv":3,"q":"In Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu, which statement is correct?","o":["Magnesium is reduced because it loses electrons","Magnesium is oxidised because it loses electrons","Copper ions are oxidised because they gain electrons","Copper ions are oxidised because they lose electrons"],"a":1,"x":"Oxidation is loss of electrons and reduction is gain. Mg loses two electrons and is oxidised, and Cu²⁺ gains two electrons and is reduced.","w":["Reduction is gain of electrons, so losing them means oxidation.","","Copper ions gain electrons, so they are reduced, not oxidised.","Copper ions gain electrons rather than lose them, so they are reduced."]}
{"id":"gch-048","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.5","lv":3,"q":"Molten lead(II) bromide is electrolysed. Which half equation happens at the cathode?","o":["Pb²⁺ → Pb + 2e⁻","Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb","2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻","Br₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Br⁻"],"a":1,"x":"Positive lead ions move to the cathode and gain electrons, so they are reduced to lead metal. Bromide ions lose electrons at the anode.","w":["This shows electrons lost, which is oxidation, not what happens at the cathode.","","Bromide ions lose electrons at the anode, not the cathode.","The melt has Br⁻ ions, not Br₂; bromine forms at the anode, not the cathode."]}
{"id":"gch-049","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.3","lv":3,"q":"H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. Bond energies in kJ/mol: H–H 436, Cl–Cl 243, H–Cl 432. What is the overall energy change?","o":["+185 kJ/mol","−679 kJ/mol","−185 kJ/mol","−864 kJ/mol"],"a":2,"x":"Energy to break bonds = 436 + 243 = 679. Energy released making bonds = 2 × 432 = 864. Overall change = 679 − 864 = −185 kJ/mol, so it is exothermic.","w":["Sign flipped: took bonds made minus bonds broken, so exothermic looks endothermic.","Used only bonds broken (436 + 243); you must also subtract the 2 × 432 released.","","Only used energy released making bonds (2 × 432) and ignored the energy to break."]}
{"id":"gch-050","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":3,"q":"N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) is endothermic in the forward direction. What happens if the temperature is raised?","o":["The equilibrium shifts left, giving more N₂O₄","The equilibrium shifts right, giving more NO₂","The position stays the same, only the rate changes","Both reactions stop until it is cooled"],"a":1,"x":"Raising the temperature favours the endothermic direction, which opposes the change. The forward reaction is endothermic, so more NO₂ forms.","w":["Shifted the wrong way: heat favours the endothermic direction, not the exothermic one.","","Temperature changes the equilibrium position, not just the rate of reaction.","Reactions never stop at equilibrium; both carry on at equal rates."]}
{"id":"gch-051","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A compound has the formula MgCl₂. What does this formula tell you?","o":["It is a mixture of magnesium and chlorine","It has two magnesium atoms for each chlorine atom","It has one magnesium atom for every two chlorine atoms, bonded together","It is an element made of one magnesium and one chlorine atom"],"a":2,"x":"The small 2 after Cl applies only to chlorine. So there is one Mg for every two Cl, chemically bonded in a fixed ratio. A mixture is not bonded. The trap is reading the number as belonging to the wrong element.","w":["A formula with a fixed ratio shows a compound, not a mixture; the elements are chemically bonded, not just mixed.","This swaps the ratio; the subscript 2 comes after Cl, so it is chlorine that has two atoms.","","Two different elements joined together make a compound, not an element; the 2 also shows there are two Cl atoms."]}
{"id":"gch-052","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Ethanol and water are mixed together and mix completely. They have different boiling points. Which method best separates them?","o":["Filtration","Crystallisation","Paper chromatography","Fractional distillation"],"a":3,"x":"Fractional distillation separates liquids that mix, using their different boiling points. Filtration only works for an insoluble solid in a liquid. Crystallisation is for getting a dissolved solid from a solution.","w":["Filtration removes insoluble solids from a liquid; both ethanol and water are liquids that mix, so they pass through together.","Crystallisation gets a dissolved solid out of solution; neither liquid here is a solid to crystallise.","Chromatography separates small amounts of dissolved substances like inks; it is not used to separate large volumes of liquids.",""]}
{"id":"gch-053","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Methane burns in oxygen to make carbon dioxide and water. Which symbol equation is correctly balanced?","o":["CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O","CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O","CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O","CH₄ + 3O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O"],"a":1,"x":"Left side: 1 C, 4 H, 4 O. Right side: 1 C, 4 H (2 × H₂O), and 2 + 2 = 4 O. Balance by changing big numbers in front, never the small numbers in formulae. Check every element, including oxygen.","w":["Not balanced: there are 4 H on the left but only 2 H on the right, and 2 O against 3 O.","","Hydrogen is fixed, but oxygen is not: 4 O on the left and 3 O on the right. The slip is balancing H only.","Oxygen is not balanced: 6 O atoms on the left but only 4 on the right, from over-counting O₂."]}
{"id":"gch-054","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A student runs a black ink on paper chromatography. The finished chromatogram shows three separate coloured spots. What does this show?","o":["The ink is a mixture of at least three different substances","The ink is a compound made from three elements","The ink is a pure substance that has split into three parts","The ink contains three atoms of one element"],"a":0,"x":"Each separate spot is a different substance, so the ink is a mixture. A pure substance gives only one spot. The trap is thinking chromatography splits compounds into elements; it only separates a mixture.","w":["","Chromatography separates substances in a mixture; it does not break a compound down into its elements.","A pure substance gives a single spot; several spots mean several substances, so it is a mixture.","Spots show different substances, not numbers of atoms; atoms are far too small to see on paper."]}
{"id":"gch-055","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A mixture contains iron filings, sand and salt. Which method separates all three and gets dry salt?","o":["Add water and filter. Evaporate the residue to get salt. Use a magnet on the filtrate.","Add water, then use simple distillation. Use a magnet on the water collected.","Remove the iron with a magnet. Add water and filter. Evaporate the filtrate to get salt.","Remove the iron with a magnet. Add water and filter. Evaporate the residue to get salt."],"a":2,"x":"Salt dissolves, so it passes through the filter paper as the filtrate, while sand stays behind as the residue. Evaporating the filtrate gives the salt. The trap is heating the residue, which is only sand.","w":["Salt dissolves and goes through the filter, so the residue is sand, not salt; the iron would also not be in the filtrate.","Simple distillation collects pure water and leaves sand and salt together in the flask, so they are not separated.","","The residue on the filter paper is sand, not salt; the salt dissolved and is in the filtrate."]}
{"id":"gch-056","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"In the alpha scattering experiment, most alpha particles went straight through thin gold foil. What did this show about the atom?","o":["Atoms are solid balls of positive charge","Most of an atom is empty space","Electrons are found in shells","The nucleus has a negative charge"],"a":1,"x":"Most alpha particles passed straight through, so most of the atom must be empty space. A few were deflected by a small, positive nucleus. The solid-ball idea belongs to the old plum pudding model.","w":["This is the plum pudding idea. Solid positive matter would have blocked or deflected many more alpha particles.","","Shells came from Bohr's later work. They cannot be shown by particles passing through the foil.","The nucleus is positive. Some alpha particles, which are positive, were repelled, so a negative nucleus does not fit."]}
{"id":"gch-057","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which row gives the correct relative charge and relative mass of a neutron?","o":["Relative charge +1, relative mass 1","Relative charge 0, relative mass very small","Relative charge −1, relative mass 1","Relative charge 0, relative mass 1"],"a":3,"x":"A neutron has no charge and a relative mass of 1, the same as a proton. The trap is mixing it up with the electron, which has a very small mass.","w":["This describes a proton. Neutrons are neutral, so the charge is not +1.","This mixes up neutron and electron. An electron has a very small mass, but a neutron has mass 1.","This mixes up charge and mass. A neutron is neutral, and −1 is the charge of an electron.",""]}
{"id":"gch-058","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Atom P has 8 protons and 8 neutrons. Which atom is an isotope of P?","o":["8 protons and 10 neutrons","7 protons and 9 neutrons","10 protons and 8 neutrons","16 protons and 8 neutrons"],"a":0,"x":"Isotopes have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Only 8 protons and 10 neutrons fits. Atoms with the same mass number but different protons are different elements.","w":["","This has the same mass number (16) but different protons, so it is a different element. Isotopes keep the proton number.","This has different protons, so it is a different element. Isotopes differ in neutrons, not protons.","This has different protons, so it is a different element. It seems to confuse proton number with mass number."]}
{"id":"gch-059","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Boron has two isotopes. 20% of atoms are boron-10 and 80% are boron-11. What is the relative atomic mass of boron?","o":["10.2","10.5","10.8","11.0"],"a":2,"x":"Use the percentages as weights: (10 × 20 + 11 × 80) ÷ 100 = 10.8. The trap is taking a simple mean of 10 and 11, which ignores how common each isotope is.","w":["This swaps the percentages, giving 80% of mass 10 and 20% of mass 11. The more common isotope should count more.","This is the simple mean of 10 and 11. It ignores that the isotopes are not equally abundant.","","This is just the mass of the most common isotope. Boron-10 must also be included in the average."]}
{"id":"gch-060","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.3","lv":3,"q":"Element X has two isotopes, X-79 and X-81. Its relative atomic mass is 79.9. What percentage of X atoms are X-81?","o":["50%","45%","55%","90%"],"a":1,"x":"Let the fraction of X-81 be y. Then 79(1 − y) + 81y = 79.9, so 79 + 2y = 79.9 and y = 0.45, or 45%. The traps are stopping at 0.9 or swapping the two isotopes.","w":["This would give a relative atomic mass of exactly 80. 79.9 is closer to 79, so X-79 is more common.","","This is the percentage of X-79. It swaps the two isotopes at the end.","This forgets that the isotopes differ by 2 mass units. 0.9 must be divided by 2 to get the fraction."]}
{"id":"gch-061","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.7","lv":1,"q":"An atom has the electronic structure 2,8,1. Which element is it?","o":["Lithium","Sodium","Potassium","Magnesium"],"a":1,"x":"2+8+1 = 11 electrons, so the atomic number is 11, which is sodium. The trap is picking another Group 1 element. Lithium, sodium and potassium all have 1 outer electron but different totals.","w":["Lithium is 2,1. It is also Group 1, but it has only two shells.","","Potassium is 2,8,8,1. It has four shells, not three.","Magnesium is 2,8,2. It has 2 outer electrons, not 1."]}
{"id":"gch-062","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.7","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how electrons fill the shells (energy levels) of an atom?","o":["They fill the outer shell first, then move inwards","Every shell is given the same number of electrons","They fill the second shell first, then the first shell","They fill the lowest energy level first, then the next one out"],"a":3,"x":"Electrons go into the lowest energy level, nearest the nucleus, first. When it is full, they go into the next shell out. The trap is thinking the outer shells fill first.","w":["The outer shell is the highest energy level. It fills last, not first, because electrons fill the lowest level first.","Shells hold different maximum numbers. The first holds 2 and the second holds 8, so they are not equal.","The first shell is nearest the nucleus and has the lowest energy. It fills before the second shell.",""]}
{"id":"gch-063","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.7","lv":2,"q":"An atom has the electronic structure 2,8,6. Which row gives its group and period in the periodic table?","o":["Group 6, period 3","Group 3, period 6","Group 8, period 3","Group 6, period 2"],"a":0,"x":"The outer shell has 6 electrons, so it is in Group 6. There are 3 shells, so it is in period 3. The trap is swapping the two ideas.","w":["","This swaps the two ideas. Group comes from the outer electrons (6), and period comes from the number of shells (3).","Group 8 would need 8 outer electrons. This atom's outer shell has 6, not 8.","The atom has three shells, not two, so it is in period 3, not period 2."]}
{"id":"gch-064","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.7","lv":2,"q":"A neutral atom has three occupied shells. Its outer shell contains 7 electrons. How many protons does the nucleus contain?","o":["7","9","17","25"],"a":2,"x":"The first two shells are full, with 2 and 8 electrons. The outer shell has 7. The total is 2+8+7 = 17 electrons. A neutral atom has equal protons and electrons, so 17 protons.","w":["This counts only the outer shell electrons. It ignores the electrons in the inner shells.","This is 2+7. It forgets that the full second shell holds 8 electrons.","","This is 2+8+8+7, which needs four shells, but this atom has only three."]}
{"id":"gch-065","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.7","lv":3,"q":"An atom has electrons in three shells. The third shell holds half as many electrons as the second shell. Which element is it?","o":["Magnesium","Silicon","Sulfur","Argon"],"a":1,"x":"The third shell is occupied, so the second shell is full with 8. Half of 8 is 4, so the structure is 2,8,4. That is 14 electrons, which is silicon. The trap is forgetting the second shell is full.","w":["Magnesium is 2,8,2. Its third shell has 2 electrons, which is a quarter of 8, not half.","","Sulfur is 2,8,6. Its third shell has 6 electrons, which is more than half of 8.","Argon is 2,8,8. Its third shell has the same number as the second shell, not half."]}
{"id":"gch-066","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"In the modern periodic table, the elements are arranged in order of what?","o":["Relative atomic mass, from smallest to largest","Atomic number, from smallest to largest","Number of neutrons in the nucleus","Number of electron shells, from fewest to most"],"a":1,"x":"The modern table is in order of increasing atomic number, which is the number of protons. The trap is relative atomic mass: early tables used atomic weight.","w":["Early tables like Newlands' and Mendeleev's used atomic weight, but the modern table uses atomic number.","","Neutron number varies between isotopes of the same element, so it cannot be used to order elements.","Shells match the periods, but within each period the order is still set by atomic number."]}
{"id":"gch-067","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which pair of properties is typical of non-metal elements that are solids at room temperature?","o":["Malleable and a good conductor of heat","Shiny and able to be drawn into wires","High density and makes a ringing sound when hit","Brittle and a poor conductor of electricity"],"a":3,"x":"Solid non-metals are usually brittle and poor conductors of heat and electricity. The trap is picking metal properties. Graphite conducts electricity, but it is an exception.","w":["Being malleable and a good heat conductor are metal properties, so this mixes up metals and non-metals.","Being shiny and ductile describes metals. Solid non-metals are usually dull and brittle.","High density and being sonorous (ringing) are typical metal properties, not non-metal ones.",""]}
{"id":"gch-068","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"An atom has the electronic structure 2,8,3. In which group and period of the periodic table is it found?","o":["Group 3, Period 3","Group 3, Period 2","Group 2, Period 3","Group 8, Period 3"],"a":0,"x":"The number of outer electrons (3) gives the group. The number of occupied shells (3) gives the period. The trap is swapping the two. This element is aluminium.","w":["","The group is right, but the period equals the number of occupied shells, which is 3, not 2.","The period is right, but the group equals the number of outer electrons, which is 3, not 2.","The group is the number of outer-shell electrons (3), not the 8 electrons in the full inner shell."]}
{"id":"gch-069","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Argon has a relative atomic mass of 39.9. Potassium has 39.1. In the modern table argon is placed before potassium. Why is this correct?","o":["Argon is a gas, so it goes before solid metals","Argon has fewer neutrons than potassium","Argon has atomic number 18 and potassium has 19","The table is ordered by relative atomic mass"],"a":2,"x":"The modern table is ordered by atomic number, not mass. Argon has 18 protons and potassium has 19, so argon comes first. The trap is thinking mass decides the order.","w":["The table is not ordered by state of matter. Argon comes first because of its atomic number.","Argon actually has more neutrons, and neutron number does not decide the order anyway.","","If the table were ordered by mass, potassium (39.1) would come before argon (39.9)."]}
{"id":"gch-070","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.1","lv":3,"q":"An atom of element X has 16 protons. Which row correctly gives its position, type and the ion it forms?","o":["Group 3, non-metal, forms X²⁻ by gaining 2 electrons","Group 6, non-metal, forms X²⁻ by gaining 2 electrons","Group 6, metal, forms X⁶⁺ by losing 6 electrons","Group 6, non-metal, forms X⁶⁻ by gaining 6 electrons"],"a":1,"x":"16 protons means 16 electrons, arranged 2,8,6. So X is in Group 6 and is a non-metal. It gains 2 electrons to fill its outer shell, forming X²⁻. This is sulfur.","w":["The group is the number of outer electrons (6), not the number of shells (3).","","With 6 outer electrons X is a non-metal, so it gains 2 electrons rather than losing 6.","It needs only 2 more electrons to fill its outer shell, so the ion is X²⁻, not X⁶⁻."]}
{"id":"gch-071","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Boiling points: neon −246 °C, argon −186 °C, xenon −108 °C. Which is the most likely boiling point of krypton, which sits between argon and xenon in Group 0?","o":["−200 °C","−153 °C","−100 °C","−250 °C"],"a":1,"x":"Noble gas boiling points rise down the group as relative atomic mass increases. Krypton is between argon and xenon, so its boiling point lies between −186 °C and −108 °C. The trap is reversing the trend.","w":["This is lower than argon's, which reverses the trend of boiling points rising down the group.","","This is higher than xenon's, but krypton is above xenon so its boiling point must be lower.","This is lower than neon's, which wrongly assumes boiling points fall down the group."]}
{"id":"gch-072","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Chlorine reacts with sodium to make sodium chloride. What happens to each chlorine atom?","o":["It loses one electron to form Cl⁺","It gains seven electrons to form Cl⁷⁻","It shares one pair of electrons with sodium","It gains one electron to form Cl⁻"],"a":3,"x":"Chlorine has seven outer electrons. It gains one to fill its outer shell, forming a 1− ion. Halogens form ionic compounds with metals. The trap is counting the seven electrons it already has.","w":["Metals lose electrons, but halogens gain them, so chlorine forms a negative ion, not Cl⁺.","Chlorine already has seven outer electrons, so it needs to gain only one more to fill its shell.","Sharing electron pairs makes covalent bonds, but a metal with a halogen forms an ionic compound.",""]}
{"id":"gch-073","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Which mixture will show a displacement reaction?","o":["Bromine solution and potassium chloride solution","Iodine solution and sodium bromide solution","Chlorine solution and sodium bromide solution","Iodine solution and potassium chloride solution"],"a":2,"x":"A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from its salt. Reactivity falls down Group 7, so chlorine is more reactive than bromine and displaces it. The trap is getting the direction of the trend wrong.","w":["Bromine is less reactive than chlorine, so it cannot displace chlorine from potassium chloride.","Iodine is less reactive than bromine, so it cannot displace bromine from sodium bromide.","","Iodine is much less reactive than chlorine, so it cannot displace chlorine from potassium chloride."]}
{"id":"gch-074","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A small piece of potassium reacts with water. Which row gives the pH of the solution left and the gas made?","o":["pH about 13, hydrogen","pH about 1, hydrogen","pH about 13, oxygen","pH about 7, hydrogen"],"a":0,"x":"Potassium reacts with water to make potassium hydroxide solution and hydrogen gas. Hydroxide ions make the solution strongly alkaline, so the pH is about 13. The trap is thinking a metal and water makes an acid.","w":["","Potassium hydroxide is alkaline, so pH 1 wrongly assumes a metal reacting with water makes an acid.","The gas released is hydrogen, because the oxygen from water ends up in the hydroxide.","The solution is not neutral, because the potassium hydroxide formed makes it alkaline."]}
{"id":"gch-075","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.4","lv":3,"q":"Astatine is below iodine in Group 7. Which prediction about astatine is most likely to be correct?","o":["It is a gas at room temperature and more reactive than iodine","It is a liquid and has a lower boiling point than iodine","It is more reactive than iodine and displaces it from potassium iodide","It is a solid at room temperature and has a higher boiling point than iodine"],"a":3,"x":"Down Group 7, melting and boiling points rise and reactivity falls. Iodine is already a solid, so astatine is a solid with a higher boiling point and is less reactive. The trap is applying Group 1 trends to the halogens.","w":["Boiling points rise and reactivity falls down Group 7, so astatine is neither a gas nor more reactive.","Boiling points rise down Group 7, so astatine's boiling point is higher than iodine's, not lower.","Reactivity decreases down Group 7, so astatine is less reactive than iodine and cannot displace it.",""]}
{"id":"gch-076","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about compounds of transition metals is correct?","o":["They are usually white, as Group 1 compounds are","They are often coloured, such as blue copper compounds","They are black because the metals are grey","They are never coloured, but their solutions are"],"a":1,"x":"Transition metal compounds are often coloured, for example blue copper(II) compounds. Group 1 compounds are usually white or colourless. The colour of the metal itself does not decide the colour of its compounds.","w":["This mixes up the groups, because Group 1 compounds are usually white but transition metal compounds are often coloured.","","Compound colour does not come from the grey metal, since copper compounds are blue and iron(III) compounds are orange-brown.","Transition metal compounds are often coloured both as solids and in solution, so 'never coloured' is wrong."]}
{"id":"gch-077","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Iron is used as a catalyst in the Haber process to make ammonia. What does the iron do?","o":["It speeds up the reaction and is not used up","It is a reactant and is used up making ammonia","It makes the reaction go faster but is used up","It makes the reaction release more energy overall"],"a":0,"x":"A catalyst speeds up a reaction and is not used up, so it can be used again. Iron is a typical transition metal catalyst. It is not a reactant and does not change the energy released.","w":["","Iron is not a reactant, because ammonia is made from nitrogen and hydrogen only and the iron is not used up.","This treats the catalyst like a reactant, but a catalyst is not used up in the reaction.","A catalyst does not change the overall energy released; it only changes how fast the reaction happens."]}
{"id":"gch-078","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Potassium reacts vigorously with cold water. What would you expect to see when a piece of copper, a transition metal, is put in cold water?","o":["It fizzes quickly and releases hydrogen like potassium","It melts and floats on the water","It reacts fast to form an alkaline solution","There is no visible reaction"],"a":3,"x":"Transition metals are much less reactive than Group 1 metals. Copper does not react with cold water. Group 1 metals such as potassium give fizzing, an alkaline solution and heat; assuming all metals behave alike is the trap.","w":["This wrongly treats all metals as similar, since quick fizzing shows a very reactive metal and copper is far less reactive.","Melting and floating are typical of Group 1 metals with low melting points and densities, which copper does not have.","An alkaline solution forms when Group 1 metals react with water, but copper does not react, so none forms.",""]}
{"id":"gch-079","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Iron is a transition metal and can form more than one ion. Iron(II) chloride is FeCl₂. What is the formula of iron(III) chloride, which contains Fe³⁺ ions?","o":["Fe₃Cl","FeCl₂","FeCl₃","Fe₂Cl₃"],"a":2,"x":"Fe³⁺ needs three Cl⁻ ions to balance the charge, so the formula is FeCl₃. The number in brackets is the ion's charge, not a subscript on iron. Transition metals can form ions with different charges.","w":["The charge number has been wrongly used as a subscript on iron, but one Fe³⁺ needs three Cl⁻ ions.","This is iron(II) chloride, which contains Fe²⁺ with two Cl⁻ ions, not Fe³⁺.","","This puts the 2 and 3 in the wrong places, because one Fe³⁺ ion is balanced by three Cl⁻ ions."]}
{"id":"gch-080","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.3","lv":3,"q":"Which unknown metal is most likely to be a transition metal?","o":["m.p. 1455 °C, 8.9 g/cm³, hard, green compounds, no reaction with cold water","m.p. 660 °C, 2.7 g/cm³, fairly soft, white compounds, no reaction with cold water","m.p. 842 °C, 1.55 g/cm³, quite hard, white compounds, reacts with cold water","m.p. 98 °C, 0.97 g/cm³, soft, white compounds, reacts quickly with cold water"],"a":0,"x":"Transition metals have high melting points and densities, are hard, do not react with cold water and often form coloured compounds. Only the first metal has all of these. A high melting point alone is not enough.","w":["","Its melting point is fairly high, but low density, softness and white compounds match aluminium, not a transition metal.","It is harder than Group 1 metals, but low density, white compounds and reaction with water match calcium.","Softness, low melting point, low density and quick reaction with water are Group 1 properties, like sodium."]}
{"id":"gch-081","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes metallic bonding?","o":["Positive ions and negative ions attract each other in a regular lattice","Atoms share pairs of electrons so each atom has a full outer shell","Positive metal ions are held together by attraction to delocalised electrons","Metal atoms transfer outer electrons to non-metal atoms to form ions"],"a":2,"x":"A metal is a lattice of positive ions in a sea of delocalised electrons. The electrostatic attraction between them is the metallic bond. Do not mix it up with ionic or covalent bonding.","w":["This describes ionic bonding, but a metal contains only positive ions and delocalised electrons, not negative ions.","This describes covalent bonding between non-metal atoms, whereas metal atoms pool their outer electrons rather than sharing pairs.","","This describes how ionic bonds form, but a pure metal has no non-metal atoms to transfer electrons to."]}
{"id":"gch-082","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Magnesium reacts with chlorine to make magnesium chloride, MgCl₂. What happens to the electrons?","o":["One magnesium atom loses two electrons and two chlorine atoms each gain one","One magnesium atom gains two electrons and two chlorine atoms each lose one","One magnesium atom shares one electron with each of two chlorine atoms","One magnesium atom loses two electrons and one chlorine atom gains both"],"a":0,"x":"Metals lose electrons and non-metals gain them. Mg loses 2 to form Mg²⁺. Each Cl needs only 1 to form Cl⁻, so two chlorine atoms are needed. The trap is thinking one Cl can take both.","w":["","This reverses the roles, because metals lose electrons to form positive ions and non-metals gain electrons to form negative ions.","This describes covalent sharing, but a metal and a non-metal bond by transferring electrons to form ions.","A chlorine atom has seven outer electrons so gains only one, forming Cl⁻, never Cl²⁻."]}
{"id":"gch-083","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"In a dot and cross diagram of ammonia, NH₃, how many outer electrons on the nitrogen atom are not in a shared pair?","o":["3","5","0","2"],"a":3,"x":"Nitrogen has 5 outer electrons. Three are shared in three N–H bonds, so 5 − 3 = 2 remain. They form one lone pair. The trap is forgetting that not every outer electron is used in bonds.","w":["Three is the number of N–H bonds, not the number of nitrogen's outer electrons left unshared.","Five is nitrogen's total outer electrons, but three of these are shared in the N–H bonds.","Zero would mean every outer electron is shared, but nitrogen forms only three bonds with its five outer electrons.",""]}
{"id":"gch-084","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Which pair of ions has the same electronic structure as each other?","o":["Na⁺ and Cl⁻","Mg²⁺ and O²⁻","Li⁺ and F⁻","K⁺ and O²⁻"],"a":1,"x":"Mg²⁺ is 2,8 after losing two outer electrons. O²⁻ is 2,8 after gaining two. Ions often match a noble gas, but you must work out each structure, not assume pairs are equal.","w":["Na⁺ is 2,8 but Cl⁻ is 2,8,8, so they have different numbers of electrons.","","Li⁺ is 2 but F⁻ is 2,8, so they match different noble gases.","K⁺ is 2,8,8 but O²⁻ is 2,8, so they match different noble gases."]}
{"id":"gch-085","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Carbon dioxide, CO₂, has two C=O double bonds. How many electrons are shared in total in one molecule?","o":["4","6","8","16"],"a":2,"x":"Each double bond is two shared pairs, so 4 shared electrons. There are two double bonds, so 2 × 4 = 8 shared electrons. The trap is counting pairs instead of electrons, or counting all outer electrons.","w":["Four is the number of shared pairs, or the electrons in one double bond, not the total shared electrons.","Six is the outer electrons on one oxygen atom, not the electrons shared in the two double bonds.","","Sixteen counts all outer electrons (4 + 6 + 6), including the oxygen lone pairs that are not shared."]}
{"id":"gch-086","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Ice melts to form liquid water. Which statement about the particles is correct?","o":["The covalent bonds inside each water molecule break","The water molecules get bigger as they gain energy","The forces between the water molecules are partly overcome","The water molecules stop moving and spread apart"],"a":2,"x":"Melting overcomes some of the forces between whole molecules. The molecules themselves stay the same. The trap is thinking the bonds inside molecules break. That only happens in chemical reactions.","w":["Melting is a physical change, so covalent bonds inside molecules stay intact and only the forces between molecules are overcome.","Molecules do not change size when ice melts; they gain energy and move more, but each molecule stays the same size.","","Particles in a liquid keep moving and stay close together; this mixes up liquids with gases and forgets that particles never stop."]}
{"id":"gch-087","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Which substance is a liquid at 20 °C? A: mp 801, bp 1413. B: mp −115, bp −85. C: mp 63, bp 290. D: mp −39, bp 357. (mp and bp in °C)","o":["Substance A","Substance B","Substance C","Substance D"],"a":3,"x":"A liquid at 20 °C must have a melting point below 20 °C and a boiling point above 20 °C. Only D fits. The trap is checking only the melting point, which makes B look possible.","w":["A melts at 801 °C, so at 20 °C it is below its melting point and is still a solid.","B melts below 20 °C but also boils at −85 °C, so at 20 °C it is a gas; only the melting point was checked.","C melts at 63 °C, which is above 20 °C, so at 20 °C it has not melted and is a solid.",""]}
{"id":"gch-088","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Magnesium ribbon is added to dilute hydrochloric acid. Magnesium chloride solution and hydrogen gas form. Which equation has the correct state symbols?","o":["Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)","Mg(s) + 2HCl(l) → MgCl₂(s) + H₂(g)","Mg(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(aq)","Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(l)"],"a":0,"x":"Magnesium ribbon is a solid (s). Dilute acid and the salt solution are dissolved in water (aq). Hydrogen escapes as a gas (g). The trap is writing (l) for any liquid-looking mixture.","w":["","Dilute acid and the salt solution are dissolved in water, so both are (aq), not (l) or (s).","Magnesium ribbon is a solid and hydrogen bubbles off as a gas; this treats everything as dissolved in water.","Hydrogen is a gas at room temperature and bubbles out, so it is (g); bubbles in a liquid are not (l)."]}
{"id":"gch-089","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"The simple particle model shows particles as small solid spheres. Which is a limitation of this model?","o":["It shows particles in a solid vibrating about fixed positions","It does not show the forces between the particles","It cannot explain why gases can be compressed","It shows particles in a gas moving randomly"],"a":1,"x":"The model draws particles as hard spheres with no forces between them. In reality, forces hold particles together. The other options are things the model shows or explains correctly, so they are not limitations.","w":["Solid particles really do vibrate about fixed positions, so showing this is a strength of the model, not a limitation.","","The model does explain compression: gas particles are far apart, so there is space to push them closer together.","Gas particles really do move randomly, so the model shows this correctly and it is not a limitation."]}
{"id":"gch-090","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A pure liquid is heated steadily. Its temperature stays constant while it boils. What explains this?","o":["The heater stops transferring energy to the liquid","The particles stop moving while they change state","The energy is used to overcome forces between particles, not to speed them up","The energy is used to break the covalent bonds inside the molecules"],"a":2,"x":"During boiling, the energy supplied is used to overcome the forces between particles. It does not increase their average kinetic energy, so the temperature stays constant. The trap is thinking the bonds inside molecules break.","w":["The heater keeps transferring energy the whole time; the energy goes into changing state rather than raising the temperature.","Particles never stop moving; this confuses no change in temperature with no movement of the particles.","","Boiling overcomes forces between molecules, not covalent bonds inside them; this confuses a change of state with a chemical reaction."]}
{"id":"gch-096","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Diamond is one of the hardest materials known. Which statement explains why?","o":["Its layers of atoms slide over each other easily","Its delocalised electrons hold the atoms tightly together","Each carbon atom forms four strong covalent bonds in a rigid lattice","Strong ionic bonds hold its carbon ions together"],"a":2,"x":"Each carbon atom in diamond bonds to four others in a giant covalent lattice. Many strong bonds must be broken to scratch it. Diamond has no layers, no ions and no delocalised electrons.","w":["Sliding layers describe graphite, not diamond. Diamond is a 3D lattice, so this mixes up the two structures.","Diamond has no delocalised electrons because all four outer electrons are used in bonds.","","Diamond contains only carbon atoms joined by covalent bonds. Carbon does not form ions here, so this confuses ionic and covalent bonding."]}
{"id":"gch-097","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Graphite is used as a lubricant and in pencils. Why can the layers in graphite slide over each other?","o":["The covalent bonds inside each layer are weak","Ions between the layers are free to move","Delocalised electrons between layers push the atoms apart","Only weak intermolecular forces act between the layers"],"a":3,"x":"Graphite has layers of carbon atoms joined by strong covalent bonds. Between the layers there are only weak intermolecular forces, so the layers slide easily. The trap is thinking the bonds within layers are weak.","w":["The covalent bonds inside layers are strong. Confusing them with the weak forces between layers is the slip here.","Graphite has no ions. It is a giant covalent structure, so this confuses it with ionic compounds.","Delocalised electrons carry charge along the layers. They do not push layers apart, and this is not why layers slide.",""]}
{"id":"gch-098","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Graphite is soft, but it has a very high melting point. Which statement explains the high melting point?","o":["Melting needs a lot of energy to break the strong covalent bonds in its layers","Melting needs a lot of energy to overcome the weak forces between layers","Melting needs a lot of energy to remove its delocalised electrons","Melting needs a lot of energy to break strong ionic bonds between its layers"],"a":0,"x":"To melt graphite, the giant covalent structure must break down. That means breaking many strong covalent bonds, which needs a lot of energy. Softness comes from weak forces between layers, which is a different point.","w":["","Weak forces between layers are easy to overcome, which is why graphite is soft. Melting needs the covalent bonds broken.","Delocalised electrons are not removed when a substance melts. They are not what holds the structure together.","Graphite has no ionic bonds. It contains only carbon atoms joined by covalent bonds."]}
{"id":"gch-099","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Graphene is a single layer of carbon atoms. It conducts electricity and is very strong. Which pair of reasons is correct?","o":["Ions move to carry charge; weak forces between layers make it strong","Delocalised electrons carry charge; strong covalent bonds make it strong","Every outer electron is in a bond; delocalised electrons make it strong","Delocalised electrons carry charge; forces between layers make it strong"],"a":1,"x":"Each carbon atom in graphene bonds to three others, leaving one delocalised electron that carries charge. The strong covalent bonds make it strong. Graphene is only one layer, so forces between layers do not apply.","w":["Graphene has no free ions, and weak forces would make it weak, not strong. Both parts are wrong.","","If every outer electron were in a bond, nothing could carry charge. That describes diamond, not graphene.","The conduction reason is right, but graphene is one layer thick. Its strength comes from covalent bonds, not forces between layers."]}
{"id":"gch-100","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.3","lv":3,"q":"An engineer needs a very thin, strong material that carries electric current along its length. Which is the best choice, and why?","o":["Diamond, as each carbon forms four strong covalent bonds","Graphite, as its layers slide easily and carry charge","Carbon nanotube, as it has strong bonds and delocalised electrons","Buckminsterfullerene, as it forms a giant covalent lattice"],"a":2,"x":"Nanotubes are cylinders of carbon atoms with strong covalent bonds, so they have high tensile strength. Each carbon has a delocalised electron, so they conduct. Diamond has no free electrons. C₆₀ is a separate molecule.","w":["Diamond is strong but has no delocalised electrons, so it does not conduct. The slip is only judging strength.","Sliding layers make graphite weak in bulk, not strong. Sliding is not a useful property for a strong wire.","","Buckminsterfullerene is made of separate C₆₀ molecules, not a giant lattice. This describes the structure wrongly."]}
{"id":"gch-101","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which size range describes nanoparticles?","o":["1 nm to 100 nm","100 nm to 2500 nm","2500 nm to 10 000 nm","0.1 nm to 1 nm"],"a":0,"x":"Nanoparticles are between 1 and 100 nm across. They contain only a few hundred atoms. Particles of 100 to 2500 nm are fine particles, and larger ones are coarse particles.","w":["","This is the range for fine particles (PM2.5), which are bigger than nanoparticles. The slip is mixing up the particle size categories.","This is the range for coarse particles (PM10), which are much bigger than nanoparticles. The slip is mixing up the categories.","This range is about the size of a single atom, far smaller than a nanoparticle, which has a few hundred atoms."]}
{"id":"gch-102","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A nanoparticle catalyst works better than the same mass of larger catalyst pieces. What is the main reason?","o":["Its atoms are lighter, so they move faster","Its bonds are stronger, so it lasts longer","It has a smaller surface area, so it is used up less","It has a much larger surface area to volume ratio"],"a":3,"x":"Nanoparticles have a very large surface area to volume ratio. More atoms are on the surface, so more reactant particles can reach them. The trap is thinking the atoms change. They stay the same.","w":["The atoms are the same as in larger pieces and are not lighter. The slip is thinking nanoparticles are made of different atoms.","The bonding is not stronger in nanoparticles. The benefit comes from surface area, not bond strength.","The same mass of nanoparticles has a larger surface area, not a smaller one, and catalysts are not used up.",""]}
{"id":"gch-103","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A dust particle has a diameter of 0.002 mm. Use 1 mm = 1 000 000 nm. How is the particle classed?","o":["Coarse particle, because it is over 10 000 nm","Fine particle, because it is between 100 nm and 2500 nm","A particle smaller than a nanoparticle, below 1 nm","Nanoparticle, because it is between 1 nm and 100 nm"],"a":1,"x":"0.002 mm × 1 000 000 = 2000 nm. This is between 100 nm and 2500 nm, so it is a fine particle. The trap is using the wrong conversion between mm and nm.","w":["0.002 mm is 2000 nm, not over 10 000 nm. The slip is adding an extra zero in the conversion.","","Converting mm to nm makes the number bigger, giving 2000 nm, not a value below 1 nm.","This uses 1 mm = 1000 nm, giving 2 nm. That factor actually converts mm to micrometres."]}
{"id":"gch-104","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Some sun creams contain nanoparticles of titanium dioxide. Which statement gives a risk that scientists raise about this use?","o":["Nanoparticles are radioactive, so they damage skin","Nanoparticles always react with water and make acid","They may enter cells, and long-term effects are not fully known","Nanoparticles cannot block UV light, so they are useless"],"a":2,"x":"Nanoparticles are so small that they might enter the body and cells. Their long-term effects are not yet fully known. The trap is inventing a property such as radioactivity. Size is the concern.","w":["Nanoparticles are not radioactive because of their size. The slip is confusing 'nano' with something harmful like radiation.","Nanoparticles do not always react with water. This is an invented property, not a real concern about nanoparticles.","","Titanium dioxide nanoparticles do block UV light and give good skin coverage. This is why they are used in sun creams."]}
{"id":"gch-105","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A catalyst is made of cube-shaped particles of side 100 nm. The same mass is remade as cubes of side 10 nm. By what factor does the total surface area increase?","o":["100 times","10 times","1000 times","It stays the same"],"a":1,"x":"Cube SA:V = 6 ÷ side. Going from 100 nm to 10 nm raises the ratio 10 times. The total volume (same mass) is unchanged, so the total surface area rises 10 times. Not 100 or 1000.","w":["Each cube's area falls 100 times, but there are 1000 times more cubes, so total area rises only 10 times.","","1000 is the rise in the number of cubes, but each small cube has 100 times less area.","The mass is the same but the surface area is not. Smaller particles expose far more surface in total."]}
{"id":"gch-106","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Why is the total mass the same before and after a reaction in a sealed container?","o":["Atoms change into other elements that have the same total mass","The number of molecules stays the same during the reaction","No atoms are made or lost. They are only rearranged into new substances","Gases have no mass, so they cannot change the total"],"a":2,"x":"Atoms are not created or destroyed in a chemical reaction. They are rearranged, so the total mass stays the same. The number of molecules can change, which is a common trap.","w":["Atoms do not change into other elements in a chemical reaction; this confuses reactions with nuclear change.","The number of molecules often changes in a reaction, so this is wrong. Mass is conserved because the atoms are.","","Gases do have mass. Thinking they do not is a common slip when a gas escapes from a flask."]}
{"id":"gch-107","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A student heats magnesium ribbon in an open crucible. The white powder left has a bigger mass than the ribbon. Why does the mass go up?","o":["Magnesium joins with oxygen from the air, so the oxygen's mass is added","Heating makes each magnesium atom heavier","Mass is created during the reaction","Magnesium oxide is a new substance, so mass is not conserved here"],"a":0,"x":"Magnesium reacts with oxygen gas from the air. The oxide contains that extra oxygen, so the solid weighs more. Mass is still conserved overall. The trap is forgetting the gas reactant.","w":["","Atoms do not gain mass when heated. The extra mass comes from oxygen atoms joining the magnesium.","Mass is never created. The extra mass was in the air as oxygen, which is easy to forget because it is a gas.","The law of conservation of mass applies to every reaction. The total mass of reactants equals the total mass of products."]}
{"id":"gch-108","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"10.0 g of calcium carbonate is heated in an open test tube: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. 5.6 g of solid CaO is left. What mass of CO₂ escaped?","o":["15.6 g","10.0 g","5.6 g","4.4 g"],"a":3,"x":"Mass is conserved, so the CO₂ mass is the starting mass minus the solid left: 10.0 − 5.6 = 4.4 g. The trap is adding the masses together instead of subtracting.","w":["This adds the two masses. The mass of CO₂ is the missing part of the 10.0 g, so you subtract.","This is the mass of calcium carbonate at the start, not the mass of gas lost.","This is the mass of calcium oxide left in the tube, not the CO₂ that escaped.",""]}
{"id":"gch-110","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Butane burns: C₄H₁₀ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Balanced using the smallest whole numbers, what is the number in front of O₂?","o":["9","26","13","6.5"],"a":2,"x":"For 1 butane you need 4 CO₂ and 5 H₂O, which is 13 oxygen atoms, or 6.5 O₂. Doubling everything gives 2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O. The O₂ number is 13.","w":["This adds the 4 carbon atoms and 5 water molecules instead of counting oxygen atoms on the right.","This is the number of oxygen atoms needed, not O₂ molecules; each O₂ has two atoms.","","This balances one butane but is not a whole number. Double the whole equation to remove the half."]}
{"id":"gch-111","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the relative formula mass (Mr) of calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂? (Ar: Ca = 40, O = 16, H = 1)","o":["57","58","74","114"],"a":2,"x":"The 2 outside the bracket multiplies everything inside. Mr = 40 + 2 × (16 + 1) = 40 + 34 = 74. The trap is doubling only the H or ignoring the 2.","w":["Ignored the 2 outside the bracket and added 40 + 16 + 1.","Doubled only the hydrogen (40 + 16 + 2); the 2 applies to both O and H.","","Doubled the whole formula including Ca; the 2 only applies to the OH inside the bracket."]}
{"id":"gch-112","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the percentage by mass of magnesium in magnesium oxide, MgO? (Ar: Mg = 24, O = 16)","o":["40%","60%","24%","150%"],"a":1,"x":"Mr of MgO = 24 + 16 = 40. Percentage of Mg = 24 ÷ 40 × 100 = 60%. The trap is dividing by the wrong mass or giving the mass as the percentage.","w":["This is the percentage of oxygen (16 ÷ 40), not magnesium.","","Gave the Ar of Mg as the percentage without dividing by the Mr.","Divided 24 by 16 (the other element) instead of by the Mr of 40."]}
{"id":"gch-113","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"What is the percentage by mass of carbon in ethanol, C₂H₅OH? (Ar: C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","o":["52.2%","26.1%","53.3%","60.0%"],"a":0,"x":"Count 6 hydrogen atoms (5 + 1). Mr = 24 + 6 + 16 = 46. Carbon = 24 ÷ 46 × 100 = 52.2%. The traps are missing the OH hydrogen and using one carbon.","w":["","Used one carbon atom (12 ÷ 46); the formula has two carbon atoms.","Counted only 5 hydrogen atoms, missing the H in OH, so Mr came out as 45.","Left out the hydrogen atoms completely, using 24 ÷ 40."]}
{"id":"gch-114","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"What is the relative formula mass (Mr) of aluminium sulfate, Al₂(SO₄)₃? (Ar: Al = 27, S = 32, O = 16)","o":["150","288","315","342"],"a":3,"x":"Al: 2 × 27 = 54. Sulfate: 32 + 64 = 96, and there are 3 of them, so 288. Mr = 54 + 288 = 342. The trap is missing a multiplier.","w":["Ignored the 3 outside the bracket and added 54 + 96.","Found only the three sulfate groups (3 × 96) and forgot the aluminium.","Used only one Al atom (27 + 288); the subscript 2 means two Al atoms.",""]}
{"id":"gch-115","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A metal oxide, M₂O₃, is 30% oxygen by mass. What is the relative atomic mass (Ar) of metal M? (Ar of O = 16)","o":["112","160","56","48"],"a":2,"x":"Oxygen = 3 × 16 = 48, which is 30% of Mr, so Mr = 48 ÷ 0.30 = 160. Two M atoms = 160 − 48 = 112, so one M = 56. The trap is stopping at 112 or 160.","w":["This is the mass of both M atoms; it must be divided by 2 because the formula has M₂.","This is the Mr of the whole oxide, not the Ar of M.","","This is the total mass of the oxygen atoms, not the metal."]}
{"id":"gch-116","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A student weighs the same sample four times and gets 12.4 g, 12.9 g, 12.6 g and 12.5 g. What is the range of these results?","o":["0.25 g","12.6 g","0.5 g","25.3 g"],"a":2,"x":"Range = largest − smallest = 12.9 − 12.4 = 0.5 g. The trap is giving the mean (12.6 g) or half the range, which is the uncertainty, not the range.","w":["That is half the range, which is the uncertainty, not the range itself.","That is the mean, not the range. The range is the largest value minus the smallest.","","Added the largest and smallest values instead of subtracting them."]}
{"id":"gch-117","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Four titration volumes are 21.40, 21.50, 22.80 and 21.60 cm³. The 22.80 cm³ value is anomalous. What are the mean and uncertainty (half the range) of the other results?","o":["21.50 ± 0.10 cm³","21.83 ± 0.70 cm³","21.50 ± 0.70 cm³","21.50 ± 0.20 cm³"],"a":0,"x":"Leave out the anomalous result. Mean = (21.40 + 21.50 + 21.60) ÷ 3 = 21.50 cm³. Range = 0.20, so uncertainty = ± 0.10 cm³.","w":["","Included the anomalous result in both the mean and the range, which spoils both values.","Left the anomaly out of the mean but still used it for the range.","Used the whole range as the uncertainty instead of half the range."]}
{"id":"gch-118","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A student finds the mass of magnesium oxide made is 0.82 ± 0.03 g. Between which two values is the true mass likely to lie?","o":["0.79 g and 0.82 g","0.79 g and 0.85 g","0.82 g and 0.85 g","0.76 g and 0.88 g"],"a":1,"x":"The ± value applies both ways. 0.82 − 0.03 = 0.79 g and 0.82 + 0.03 = 0.85 g. The true value is likely to lie between these.","w":["Only subtracted the uncertainty; it also needs to be added.","","Only added the uncertainty; it also needs to be subtracted.","Doubled the uncertainty, using ± 0.06 instead of ± 0.03."]}
{"id":"gch-119","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Student A's repeat volumes are 15.2, 15.6 and 15.4 cm³. Student B's are 15.3, 15.4 and 15.5 cm³. Using half the range as uncertainty, which statement is correct?","o":["A has the smaller uncertainty because its readings are more spread out","A and B have the same uncertainty because both means are 15.4 cm³","B has the larger range, so B's results are less precise","B has the smaller uncertainty: ± 0.1 cm³ compared with ± 0.2 cm³ for A"],"a":3,"x":"Both means are 15.4 cm³. A's range is 0.4 so ± 0.2. B's range is 0.2 so ± 0.1. Equal means do not mean equal uncertainty; a smaller range means a smaller uncertainty.","w":["A wider spread gives a larger uncertainty, not a smaller one.","Uncertainty depends on the range, not the mean. The ranges are different, so the uncertainties are different.","B's range (0.2) is smaller than A's (0.4), so B's results are closer together.",""]}
{"id":"gch-120","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A student reports a mean volume of 18.40 ± 0.15 cm³ from three repeats. Uncertainty is half the range. Which set of three readings gives this result?","o":["18.30, 18.40, 18.50 cm³","18.10, 18.40, 18.70 cm³","18.25, 18.40, 18.55 cm³","18.25, 18.40, 18.70 cm³"],"a":2,"x":"An uncertainty of ± 0.15 means a range of 0.30. 18.25, 18.40, 18.55 has range 0.30 and mean 18.40. The trap is forgetting to double the uncertainty, or getting the wrong mean.","w":["Range is 0.20, which gives ± 0.10, not ± 0.15.","Range is 0.60, which gives ± 0.30. This forgets that uncertainty is half the range.","","The range is 0.45, which gives ± 0.225, and the mean is 18.45, not 18.40."]}
{"id":"gch-121","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.1","lv":3,"q":"2.4 g of magnesium is added to 0.10 mol of hydrochloric acid: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂. What mass of hydrogen forms? (Ar: Mg = 24, H = 1)","o":["0.20 g","0.10 g","0.050 g","0.40 g"],"a":1,"x":"Mg is 0.10 mol, which needs 0.20 mol HCl. Only 0.10 mol HCl is present, so HCl is limiting. The ratio HCl : H₂ is 2 : 1, so 0.050 mol H₂ forms. Mass = 0.050 × 2 = 0.10 g.","w":["Treated magnesium as the limiting reactant. 0.10 mol H₂ would need 0.20 mol HCl, which is not there.","","Gave the moles of H₂ (0.050) as a mass. You must multiply by Mr = 2.","Multiplied the HCl moles by 2 instead of dividing, so the 2 : 1 ratio was used the wrong way round."]}
{"id":"gch-122","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.1","lv":3,"q":"2.7 g of aluminium reacts exactly with 2.4 g of oxygen (O₂) to form aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃. Which balanced equation matches these masses? (Ar: Al = 27, O = 16)","o":["Al + O₂ → AlO₂","2Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃","4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃","2Al + O₂ → Al₂O₃"],"a":2,"x":"Al is 2.7 ÷ 27 = 0.10 mol. O₂ is 2.4 ÷ 32 = 0.075 mol. The ratio is 0.10 : 0.075 = 4 : 3. So 4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃, which also balances. Trap: using O atoms instead of O₂ molecules.","w":["AlO₂ is the wrong formula; aluminium oxide is Al₂O₃, and a 1 : 1 ratio does not match 4 : 3.","Not balanced: 2 Al on the left but 4 on the right, and the 2 : 3 ratio does not match 4 : 3.","","Not balanced: 2 O atoms on the left but 3 on the right, and the 2 : 1 ratio does not match 4 : 3."]}
{"id":"gch-123","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.5","lv":1,"q":"A salt solution has a concentration of 40 g/dm³. What mass of salt is dissolved in 2.0 dm³ of this solution?","o":["20 g","38 g","80 g","0.05 g"],"a":2,"x":"Mass = concentration × volume = 40 × 2.0 = 80 g. The trap is dividing instead of multiplying, or adding or subtracting the numbers.","w":["Divided 40 by 2.0; to find the mass you multiply concentration by volume.","Subtracted 2.0 from 40; the quantities must be multiplied, not subtracted.","","Divided 2.0 by 40, which turns the formula upside down."]}
{"id":"gch-124","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.5","lv":1,"q":"A student has a beaker of salt solution. Which change would increase its concentration in g/dm³ without adding any more salt?","o":["Add more water","Heat to evaporate some water","Pour away half of the solution","Stir the solution for longer"],"a":1,"x":"Evaporating water lowers the volume while the mass of salt stays the same, so the concentration goes up. Pouring away half keeps the concentration the same.","w":["Adding water increases the volume, so the same mass of salt is spread out and the concentration falls.","","Half the solution leaves with half the salt, so the concentration stays the same; this confuses amount with concentration.","Stirring only helps dissolving; it does not change the mass of salt or the volume."]}
{"id":"gch-125","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"500 cm³ of a solution has a concentration of 15 g/dm³. A student pours out 100 cm³ of it. What mass of solute is in the 100 cm³ sample?","o":["1.5 g","7.5 g","15 g","0.15 g"],"a":0,"x":"100 cm³ = 0.100 dm³. Mass = 15 × 0.100 = 1.5 g. The trap is using the whole 500 cm³ or forgetting that the sample's volume decides the mass.","w":["","This is the mass in the whole 500 cm³ (15 × 0.5); the question asks about the 100 cm³ sample only.","This is the concentration, not a mass; concentration must be multiplied by the volume in dm³.","Converted 100 cm³ to 0.01 dm³ by mistake; 100 cm³ is 0.1 dm³."]}
{"id":"gch-126","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A solution has a concentration of 25 g/dm³. What volume of this solution contains 5.0 g of the dissolved solid?","o":["0.20 cm³","125 cm³","5000 cm³","200 cm³"],"a":3,"x":"Volume = mass ÷ concentration = 5.0 ÷ 25 = 0.20 dm³. Multiply by 1000 to get 200 cm³. The trap is leaving the answer in dm³ but labelling it cm³.","w":["Found 0.20 dm³ but did not convert it to cm³; 1 dm³ is 1000 cm³.","Multiplied 25 by 5.0 instead of dividing mass by concentration.","Divided concentration by mass (25 ÷ 5.0 = 5 dm³), the wrong way round, then converted to cm³.",""]}
{"id":"gch-127","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.5","lv":3,"q":"200 cm³ of a solution has a concentration of 10 g/dm³. It is heated until only 125 cm³ is left. No solute is lost. What is the new concentration?","o":["6.25 g/dm³","10 g/dm³","16 g/dm³","0.016 g/dm³"],"a":2,"x":"Mass of solute = 10 × 0.200 = 2.0 g. New concentration = 2.0 ÷ 0.125 = 16 g/dm³. Less volume with the same mass means a higher concentration, not a lower one.","w":["Scaled 10 by 125/200, which makes the concentration fall; less water should make it rise.","Assumed the concentration is unchanged, but the volume fell while the mass stayed the same.","","Divided 2.0 g by 125 without converting cm³ to dm³, so the answer is far too small."]}
{"id":"gch-128","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.3","lv":1,"q":"A student expects to make 15.0 g of a product. She actually collects 12.0 g. What is the percentage yield?","o":["80%","125%","25%","20%"],"a":0,"x":"Percentage yield = actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100 = 12.0 ÷ 15.0 × 100 = 80%. The trap is turning the fraction upside down.","w":["","Divided the theoretical yield by the actual yield, so the fraction is upside down; the yield cannot be over 100% here.","Divided the 3.0 g lost by the actual yield; the lost mass is not the yield.","This is the percentage lost (100 − 80), not the percentage yield."]}
{"id":"gch-129","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Why do chemical companies prefer a reaction with a high atom economy?","o":["It always gives a higher percentage yield of the product","It makes the reaction go faster, so more product is made each hour","It lets the reaction run at a lower temperature, saving energy","More of the reactant atoms become useful product, so less is wasted"],"a":3,"x":"Atom economy measures how much of the reactants' mass ends up in the desired product. A high value means less waste and better use of raw materials. The trap is confusing it with yield or rate.","w":["Atom economy and percentage yield are different ideas; a reaction can have a high yield but a low atom economy.","Atom economy says nothing about speed; rate depends on things like temperature and concentration.","Atom economy does not tell you the temperature needed; it only compares product mass with reactant mass.",""]}
{"id":"gch-130","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Iron is made by: Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂. Ar: Fe = 56, C = 12, O = 16. What is the atom economy for making iron?","o":["70.0%","23.0%","45.9%","59.6%"],"a":2,"x":"Mr Fe₂O₃ = 160 and 3CO = 84, so reactants = 244. Iron made = 2 × 56 = 112. Atom economy = 112 ÷ 244 × 100 = 45.9%. The trap is ignoring the carbon monoxide.","w":["Divided by the Mr of Fe₂O₃ only (160); all the reactants must be included.","Used only one Fe (56) instead of 2Fe (112); the equation makes 2 moles of iron.","","Used one CO (28) instead of 3CO (84) in the total; the balancing numbers must be used."]}
{"id":"gch-131","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. A student burns 4.8 g of magnesium and collects 6.0 g of magnesium oxide. Ar: Mg = 24, O = 16. What is the percentage yield?","o":["75%","125%","37.5%","25%"],"a":0,"x":"Moles Mg = 4.8 ÷ 24 = 0.20, so 0.20 mol MgO (Mr 40) = 8.0 g theoretical. Yield = 6.0 ÷ 8.0 × 100 = 75%. The trap is using the mass of magnesium as the theoretical yield.","w":["","Compared the product with the mass of magnesium (6.0 ÷ 4.8); the theoretical yield is the mass of MgO expected, 8.0 g.","Used 2 × 40 = 80 g per mole of Mg, giving 16 g; 0.20 mol of Mg gives only 0.20 mol of MgO.","This is the percentage lost (100 − 75), not the percentage yield."]}
{"id":"gch-132","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.3","lv":3,"q":"25 g of CaCO₃ is heated: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. 9.8 g of CaO is made. Ar: Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16. What is the percentage yield of CaO?","o":["39.2%","70%","56%","142.9%"],"a":1,"x":"Moles CaCO₃ = 25 ÷ 100 = 0.25, so theoretical CaO = 0.25 × 56 = 14.0 g. Yield = 9.8 ÷ 14.0 × 100 = 70%. The traps are using the reactant mass or mixing up yield with atom economy.","w":["Divided by the mass of CaCO₃ (25 g); the theoretical yield must be the mass of CaO expected, 14.0 g.","","This is the atom economy (56 ÷ 100), not the percentage yield from the masses given.","Divided the theoretical yield by the actual yield (14.0 ÷ 9.8), which turns the fraction upside down."]}
{"id":"gch-133","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.4","lv":3,"q":"25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ sulfuric acid. 2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. What is the concentration of the NaOH?","o":["0.0800 mol/dm³","0.125 mol/dm³","0.160 mol/dm³","0.200 mol/dm³"],"a":2,"x":"Moles H₂SO₄ = 0.0200 × 0.100 = 0.00200. The ratio is 1:2, so NaOH = 0.00400 mol. Concentration = 0.00400 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.160 mol/dm³. The trap is missing the 1:2 ratio.","w":["Forgot the 1:2 ratio, so used 0.00200 mol of NaOH instead of 0.00400 mol.","Used only the volumes (0.100 × 25 ÷ 20). This ignores the moles and the 1:2 ratio.","","Doubled the acid concentration for the ratio but ignored the different volumes of the two solutions."]}
{"id":"gch-134","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.4","lv":3,"q":"0.54 g of aluminium (Ar = 27) reacts with excess hydrochloric acid. 2Al + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂. What volume of hydrogen forms at room temperature and pressure? (1 mole of gas = 24 dm³)","o":["720 cm³","480 cm³","320 cm³","12 960 cm³"],"a":0,"x":"Moles Al = 0.54 ÷ 27 = 0.020. The ratio Al : H₂ is 2 : 3, so H₂ = 0.030 mol. Volume = 0.030 × 24 = 0.72 dm³ = 720 cm³. The trap is using the ratio the wrong way.","w":["","Treated Al and H₂ as 1:1, giving 0.020 × 24 dm³, so the 2:3 ratio was ignored.","Used the ratio upside down (2/3 of 0.020 mol) instead of 3/2 of 0.020 mol.","Multiplied the mass 0.54 g by 24 without first converting it to moles."]}
{"id":"gch-135","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.4","lv":3,"q":"CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂. 50.0 cm³ of hydrochloric acid reacts fully with excess CaCO₃ and gives 240 cm³ of CO₂ at room temperature and pressure. What is the acid's concentration?","o":["0.200 mol/dm³","0.800 mol/dm³","4.00 mol/dm³","0.400 mol/dm³"],"a":3,"x":"Moles CO₂ = 0.240 ÷ 24 = 0.0100. The ratio HCl : CO₂ is 2 : 1, so HCl = 0.0200 mol. Concentration = 0.0200 ÷ 0.0500 = 0.400 mol/dm³. The trap is forgetting the 2:1 ratio.","w":["Forgot the 2:1 ratio, so used 0.0100 mol of HCl instead of 0.0200 mol.","Applied the 2:1 ratio twice, giving 0.0400 mol of HCl.","Converted 50.0 cm³ to 0.00500 dm³ instead of 0.0500 dm³, which makes the answer ten times too big.",""]}
{"id":"gch-136","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which metal is extracted from its ore by heating the metal oxide with carbon?","o":["Aluminium","Iron","Potassium","Calcium"],"a":1,"x":"Carbon can only take oxygen from metals less reactive than carbon. Iron is below carbon in the reactivity series. Aluminium, potassium and calcium are above carbon, so they are extracted by electrolysis.","w":["Aluminium is more reactive than carbon, so carbon cannot remove its oxygen; assuming every metal uses carbon is the slip.","","Potassium is far more reactive than carbon, so carbon cannot reduce its oxide; assuming every metal uses carbon is the slip.","Calcium is more reactive than carbon, so carbon cannot reduce its oxide; assuming every metal uses carbon is the slip."]}
{"id":"gch-137","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Three metals are put into dilute hydrochloric acid. P fizzes quickly. Q fizzes slowly. R shows no bubbles. What is the order of reactivity, most reactive first?","o":["P, Q, R","R, Q, P","Q, P, R","P, R, Q"],"a":0,"x":"The faster the fizzing, the more reactive the metal. P fizzes fastest, then Q. R does not react, so it is least reactive. The trap is reversing the order.","w":["","This reverses the order, but no reaction means least reactive, not most reactive.","This swaps P and Q, but quicker fizzing shows P is more reactive than Q.","This puts R in the middle, but a metal with no reaction is the least reactive of the three."]}
{"id":"gch-138","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Zinc oxide is heated with carbon: ZnO + C → Zn + CO. Which statement is correct?","o":["Zinc oxide is oxidised because it loses oxygen","Carbon is reduced because it gains oxygen","Carbon is oxidised because it loses oxygen","Zinc oxide is reduced because it loses oxygen"],"a":3,"x":"Reduction is loss of oxygen. Zinc oxide loses oxygen to become zinc, so it is reduced. Carbon gains oxygen to form carbon monoxide, so it is oxidised. The trap is mixing up the two words.","w":["Losing oxygen is reduction, not oxidation, so this swaps the two terms.","Gaining oxygen is oxidation, not reduction, so this swaps the two terms.","Carbon gains oxygen here rather than losing it, so this mixes up the direction of oxygen transfer.",""]}
{"id":"gch-139","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is aluminium not extracted by heating aluminium oxide with carbon?","o":["Aluminium is less reactive than carbon","Carbon is a non-metal, so it cannot react with metal oxides","Aluminium is more reactive than carbon","Aluminium oxide dissolves in water"],"a":2,"x":"A more reactive element holds on to oxygen more strongly. Aluminium is above carbon in the reactivity series, so carbon cannot take its oxygen. Aluminium is extracted by electrolysis instead.","w":["Aluminium is more reactive than carbon, not less, so this reads the reactivity series the wrong way round.","Carbon does reduce oxides of less reactive metals such as iron oxide, so being a non-metal is not the problem.","","Aluminium oxide does not dissolve in water, and solubility does not decide whether carbon can reduce an oxide."]}
{"id":"gch-140","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Metal X displaces copper from copper sulfate solution. X does not displace zinc from zinc sulfate solution. Which metal could X be?","o":["Magnesium","Iron","Silver","Gold"],"a":1,"x":"X must be more reactive than copper but less reactive than zinc. Iron lies between zinc and copper in the reactivity series. The trap is checking only one of the two results.","w":["Magnesium is more reactive than zinc, so it would displace zinc from zinc sulfate solution.","","Silver is less reactive than copper, so it could not displace copper from copper sulfate solution.","Gold is less reactive than copper, so it could not displace copper from copper sulfate solution."]}
{"id":"gch-141","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.4","lv":3,"q":"Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid: Mg + 2H⁺ + 2Cl⁻ → Mg²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ + H₂. Which statement about this reaction is correct?","o":["Magnesium atoms gain two electrons and are reduced","Each hydrogen ion gains one electron and is reduced","Each hydrogen ion loses one electron and is oxidised","Chloride ions gain electrons and are reduced to chlorine"],"a":1,"x":"Mg loses 2 electrons and is oxidised. Each H⁺ gains 1 electron to form H₂, so it is reduced. Cl⁻ ions are the same on both sides, so they are spectator ions. Do not mix up gain and loss.","w":["Magnesium becomes Mg²⁺, so it loses electrons and is oxidised; it does not gain them.","","H⁺ becomes H₂ by gaining electrons, which is reduction; losing electrons would be oxidation.","Chloride ions stay as Cl⁻ on both sides, so they are spectator ions and are not reduced."]}
{"id":"gch-142","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.4","lv":3,"q":"Chlorine water is added to potassium bromide solution. The ionic equation is Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂. Which half-equation shows oxidation?","o":["Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻","2Br⁻ + 2e⁻ → Br₂","Cl₂ → 2Cl⁻ + 2e⁻","2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻"],"a":3,"x":"Bromide ions lose electrons to form bromine, so 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻ is oxidation. In oxidation the electrons are on the right. The chlorine half-equation is reduction, with electrons on the left.","w":["Chlorine gains electrons to form chloride ions, so this half-equation shows reduction, not oxidation.","Electrons on the left would mean bromide ions gain electrons, but they lose them, and charges do not balance.","Chlorine gains electrons to form Cl⁻; electrons on the right wrongly show chlorine losing them.",""]}
{"id":"gch-143","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Magnesium is added to dilute hydrochloric acid and bubbles form. Which gas is made, and how is it identified?","o":["Carbon dioxide; it turns limewater milky","Hydrogen; it relights a glowing splint","Hydrogen; a lit splint gives a squeaky pop","Oxygen; a lit splint gives a squeaky pop"],"a":2,"x":"Acid + metal gives a salt + hydrogen. Hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop when a lit splint is held at the mouth of the tube. Relighting a glowing splint is the test for oxygen, not hydrogen.","w":["Carbon dioxide comes from carbonates reacting with acids, not metals, so this mixes up the two reactions.","A glowing splint relighting is the oxygen test, so this mixes up the gas tests.","","Oxygen is not made, and the squeaky pop is the hydrogen test, so this pairs the wrong gas with it."]}
{"id":"gch-145","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Calcium carbonate reacts with dilute nitric acid. Which row gives all the products?","o":["Calcium nitrate and hydrogen","Calcium nitrate and water","Calcium nitride, water and carbon dioxide","Calcium nitrate, water and carbon dioxide"],"a":3,"x":"Acid + carbonate gives a salt + water + carbon dioxide. Nitric acid gives a nitrate. Fizzing shows carbon dioxide is made. Hydrogen is not made from carbonates.","w":["Hydrogen only comes from metals reacting with acids, whereas a carbonate makes carbon dioxide instead.","This leaves out carbon dioxide, which carbonates always give off and which causes the fizzing.","Nitride is the wrong name because nitric acid always gives a nitrate salt.",""]}
{"id":"gch-146","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student filters off excess copper oxide, leaving blue copper sulfate solution. What is the best next step to get crystals?","o":["Heat gently to evaporate some water, then leave to cool and crystallise","Heat until all the water has boiled away","Add more sulfuric acid to react with any leftover oxide","Leave the solution in a closed bottle until crystals appear"],"a":0,"x":"Evaporate only part of the water, then cool slowly so crystals form. Boiling to dryness gives a powder and can damage the salt. Adding acid would make the solution acidic again.","w":["","Boiling to dryness gives a powder, not neat crystals, so the trap is evaporating too far.","The excess oxide is already filtered off, so extra acid would just contaminate the salt with acid.","A closed bottle stops water evaporating, so the solution stays too dilute to crystallise."]}
{"id":"gch-147","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A student makes copper sulfate crystals but gets much less mass than expected. Which action is the most likely cause?","o":["Using more excess copper oxide than was needed","Stirring the oxide and acid while warming","Washing the crystals with lots of warm water","Leaving the solution to cool slowly"],"a":2,"x":"Copper sulfate is soluble, so lots of warm water dissolves some crystals and lowers the yield. Extra insoluble oxide is filtered off, so it does not cost any salt. Slow cooling gives bigger crystals.","w":["Extra copper oxide is insoluble and filtered off, so it does not reduce the salt made.","Stirring helps the oxide react with the acid, so it helps the yield rather than reducing it.","","Slow cooling gives larger crystals but does not remove any salt from the product."]}
{"id":"gch-149","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which equation shows the ionic change that happens in any neutralisation of an acid by an alkali?","o":["H⁻(aq) + OH⁺(aq) → H₂O(l)","H⁺(aq) + O²⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)","H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)","H₂(g) + O₂(g) → H₂O(l)"],"a":2,"x":"Acids give H⁺ ions and alkalis give OH⁻ ions. They react to make water. The trap is swapping the charges or using O²⁻ instead of OH⁻.","w":["The charges are swapped, because acids contain H⁺ ions and alkalis contain OH⁻ ions.","Alkalis contain hydroxide ions, OH⁻, not oxide ions, O²⁻.","","This is hydrogen reacting with oxygen, not neutralisation, and it is not even balanced."]}
{"id":"gch-150","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A titration gives these titres in cm³: 24.80, 23.45, 23.55, 23.50. The first was a rough run. What is the mean titre of the concordant results?","o":["23.50 cm³","23.83 cm³","24.80 cm³","23.55 cm³"],"a":0,"x":"Concordant results are within 0.10 cm³ of each other: 23.45, 23.55 and 23.50. Their mean is 70.50 ÷ 3 = 23.50 cm³. The trap is averaging all four results.","w":["","This is the mean of all four results, wrongly including the rough run, which is not concordant.","This is the rough result itself, which is not concordant and is not used in the mean.","This is just one of the concordant results, not the mean of all three."]}
{"id":"gch-152","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":3,"q":"25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ sulfuric acid. 2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. What is the concentration of the NaOH?","o":["0.0400 mol/dm³","0.0800 mol/dm³","0.160 mol/dm³","0.125 mol/dm³"],"a":2,"x":"Moles of H₂SO₄ = 0.0200 × 0.100 = 0.00200. The ratio is 1 : 2, so NaOH = 0.00400 mol. Concentration = 0.00400 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.160 mol/dm³. The trap is ignoring the 1 : 2 ratio.","w":["This halves the moles of acid instead of doubling them, using the 1 : 2 ratio the wrong way round.","This treats the reaction as 1 : 1 and ignores the 2 in 2NaOH.","","This does 0.100 × 25 ÷ 20, putting the volumes upside down and ignoring the 1 : 2 ratio."]}
{"id":"gch-153","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.6","lv":3,"q":"Which statement correctly compares dilute hydrochloric acid with concentrated ethanoic acid?","o":["HCl is strong as it fully ionises; ethanoic acid is weak but has more acid per dm³","HCl is the weaker acid because it has fewer moles of acid per dm³","Ethanoic acid is the stronger acid because it is more concentrated","Both acids are equally strong, as strength depends only on concentration"],"a":0,"x":"Strength is how much an acid ionises in water. Concentration is how much acid is in each dm³. HCl is strong but dilute. Ethanoic acid is weak but concentrated. Do not mix up the two ideas.","w":["","Confuses strength with concentration. Strength depends on how much ionises, not on moles per dm³.","Confuses concentration with strength. A concentrated weak acid is still only partly ionised.","Strength does not depend on concentration; it depends on the degree of ionisation of the acid."]}
{"id":"gch-154","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.6","lv":3,"q":"25 cm³ of hydrochloric acid of pH 2 is mixed with water to make 250 cm³. HCl is a strong acid. What is the pH of the new solution?","o":["pH 2","pH 1","pH 3","pH 4"],"a":2,"x":"The volume is ten times bigger, so the H⁺ concentration is ten times smaller. A tenfold fall in H⁺ concentration raises the pH by 1, so the pH is 3. The acid is still strong, but more dilute.","w":["Thinks a strong acid keeps its pH when diluted; strength stays the same but concentration and pH change.","Lowers the pH by 1. Dilution makes the solution less acidic, so the pH goes up.","","Raises pH by 2, as if the H⁺ concentration fell 100 times; the dilution is only tenfold."]}
{"id":"gch-155","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.6","lv":3,"q":"Excess magnesium is added to equal volumes of 0.1 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid and 0.1 mol/dm³ ethanoic acid. Which statement is correct?","o":["Hydrochloric acid gives more hydrogen, as it is the stronger acid.","Ethanoic acid gives less hydrogen, as it is only partly ionised.","Both react at the same rate at first, as the concentrations are equal.","Hydrochloric acid reacts faster at first, but both give the same volume of hydrogen."],"a":3,"x":"HCl has a higher H⁺ concentration, so it reacts faster at first. Ethanoic acid ionises more as H⁺ is used up. Moles of acid are equal and magnesium is in excess, so the hydrogen volumes are equal.","w":["Strength affects rate, not the total amount of hydrogen. Equal moles of acid give equal hydrogen.","Partial ionisation is reversible, so more ionises as H⁺ is used. All the acid eventually reacts.","Equal concentration does not mean equal H⁺ concentration. The weak acid has fewer H⁺ ions, so it reacts slower.",""]}
{"id":"gch-161","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Sodium sulfate solution is electrolysed using inert electrodes. Which gas forms at the cathode, and how is it tested?","o":["Oxygen, which relights a glowing splint","Hydrogen, which gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint","Hydrogen, which relights a glowing splint","Oxygen, which gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint"],"a":1,"x":"Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so hydrogen forms at the cathode. Hydrogen gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint. Oxygen is the gas that relights a glowing splint.","w":["Oxygen forms at the anode, not the cathode, so this mixes up the two electrodes.","","The gas is right, but relighting a glowing splint is the test for oxygen, not hydrogen.","Oxygen forms at the anode, and a squeaky pop is the hydrogen test, so both parts are wrong."]}
{"id":"gch-162","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Sodium chloride solution is electrolysed using inert electrodes. What forms at the anode, and what is the test for it?","o":["Chlorine, which bleaches damp litmus paper","Chlorine, which relights a glowing splint","Oxygen, which bleaches damp litmus paper","Oxygen, which relights a glowing splint"],"a":0,"x":"A halide ion (chloride) is present, so chlorine forms at the anode. Chlorine bleaches damp litmus paper. Oxygen only forms when no halide ions are present.","w":["","The product is right, but relighting a glowing splint is the test for oxygen, not chlorine.","Chloride ions are present so chlorine forms, and bleaching litmus is the chlorine test, not oxygen's.","Oxygen only forms when there are no halide ions, but chloride ions are present here."]}
{"id":"gch-163","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Potassium bromide solution is electrolysed using inert electrodes. What forms at each electrode?","o":["Potassium at the cathode and bromine at the anode","Potassium at the cathode and oxygen at the anode","Hydrogen at the cathode and bromine at the anode","Hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode"],"a":2,"x":"Potassium is more reactive than hydrogen, so hydrogen forms at the cathode. Bromide is a halide ion, so bromine forms at the anode. Oxygen is only made when no halide ions are present.","w":["Potassium is more reactive than hydrogen, so it stays as ions and hydrogen forms at the cathode instead.","Potassium is too reactive to form, and oxygen does not form because a halide ion is present.","","Bromide is a halide ion, so bromine forms at the anode, not oxygen."]}
{"id":"gch-164","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.4","lv":3,"q":"Hydrogen ions gain electrons at the cathode to make hydrogen gas. Which half equation shows this correctly?","o":["H₂ → 2H⁺ + 2e⁻","2H⁺ → H₂ + 2e⁻","H⁺ + e⁻ → H₂","2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂"],"a":3,"x":"Reduction happens at the cathode, so the electrons are on the left. The equation must also balance in atoms and charge: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. Forgetting to balance the equation is a common slip.","w":["This shows hydrogen losing electrons, the reverse of what happens to positive ions at the cathode.","The electrons are on the wrong side, because ions gain electrons at the cathode.","This is not balanced, since two H⁺ ions and two electrons are needed to make one H₂ molecule.",""]}
{"id":"gch-165","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.4","lv":3,"q":"A solution is electrolysed with inert electrodes. The cathode gets a pink-brown coating. The gas at the anode bleaches damp litmus paper. Which solution was used?","o":["Copper sulfate","Copper chloride","Sodium chloride","Zinc chloride"],"a":1,"x":"A pink-brown coating is copper, which is less reactive than hydrogen. Bleaching gas is chlorine, from a halide. So the solution is copper chloride. Copper sulfate would give oxygen at the anode.","w":["Sulfate ions are not discharged, so oxygen would form at the anode, and oxygen does not bleach litmus.","","Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so hydrogen forms at the cathode and there is no copper coating.","Zinc is more reactive than hydrogen, so hydrogen forms at the cathode and there is no pink-brown coating."]}
{"id":"gch-166","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.5","lv":3,"q":"Aqueous copper(II) sulfate is electrolysed with inert electrodes. Oxygen forms at the anode. Which is the correct balanced half equation for this?","o":["4OH⁻ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂","4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 2e⁻","4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻","SO₄²⁻ → SO₂ + O₂ + 2e⁻"],"a":2,"x":"Hydroxide ions lose electrons at the anode, which is oxidation. Four OH⁻ carry a 4− charge, so four electrons are released to balance the charge. Trap: writing only 2e⁻, or putting the electrons on the left.","w":["Electrons on the left show gain (reduction), but the anode reaction is oxidation, so the electrons belong on the right.","The atoms balance but the charge does not: 4− on the left and only 2− on the right, so 4e⁻ are needed.","","Sulfate ions are not discharged in aqueous solution; hydroxide ions from water are oxidised to oxygen instead."]}
{"id":"gch-167","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.5","lv":3,"q":"Concentrated aqueous sodium chloride is electrolysed with inert electrodes. Which half equation happens at the cathode?","o":["2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂","Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na","2H⁺ → H₂ + 2e⁻","2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻"],"a":0,"x":"Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so H⁺ ions from water gain electrons (reduction) at the cathode and hydrogen gas forms. Trap: thinking the metal ion is always discharged.","w":["","Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so sodium is not formed from an aqueous solution; this is the 'metal always forms' slip.","Electrons on the right show loss (oxidation), but the cathode reaction needs a gain of electrons.","This is chloride being oxidised at the anode, not the cathode, which attracts positive ions."]}
{"id":"gch-168","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A student mixes citric acid solution with sodium hydrogencarbonate. The temperature falls from 21°C to 14°C. What does this show?","o":["The reaction is exothermic because energy moves to the surroundings","The reaction is endothermic because it takes in energy from the surroundings","The reaction is exothermic because it needs energy to start","The reaction is endothermic because the surroundings get hotter"],"a":1,"x":"A fall in temperature means the reaction took in energy from the surroundings, so it is endothermic. The trap is thinking a temperature fall means energy was given out.","w":["Exothermic reactions give out energy, which makes the temperature rise, not fall.","","All reactions need activation energy to start, so this does not show a reaction is exothermic.","The surroundings cool in an endothermic reaction, so saying they get hotter is wrong."]}
{"id":"gch-169","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is an exothermic reaction?","o":["Thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate","Photosynthesis in a leaf","Citric acid reacting with sodium hydrogencarbonate","Neutralisation of hydrochloric acid with sodium hydroxide"],"a":3,"x":"Neutralisation gives out energy and the temperature rises, so it is exothermic. Thermal decomposition, photosynthesis and the citric acid reaction all take in energy. The trap is assuming every reaction is exothermic.","w":["Thermal decomposition needs a constant supply of heat, so it is endothermic.","Photosynthesis takes in energy from light, so it is endothermic.","This reaction makes the temperature fall, so it is endothermic.",""]}
{"id":"gch-170","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A student does a neutralisation in a polystyrene cup with a lid. The temperature rise is smaller than the true value. What is the most likely reason?","o":["Some energy was still transferred to the surroundings","The reaction was endothermic","The lid stopped energy escaping from the cup","Polystyrene gives out energy during the reaction"],"a":0,"x":"Even with a lid, some energy escapes to the air, so the measured rise is too small. An endothermic reaction would make the temperature fall. The trap is thinking insulation removes all energy loss.","w":["","An endothermic reaction would make the temperature fall, not rise, so this does not fit the results.","The lid reduces energy loss, so it would make the rise closer to the true value, not smaller.","Polystyrene is an insulator and does not give out energy; the energy comes from the reaction."]}
{"id":"gch-171","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A reaction profile shows reactants at 300 kJ, the highest point at 450 kJ and products at 180 kJ. How much energy is given out overall?","o":["150 kJ","120 kJ","270 kJ","180 kJ"],"a":1,"x":"Overall energy change is reactants minus products: 300 − 180 = 120 kJ given out. The trap is using the activation energy (450 − 300 = 150 kJ) instead.","w":["This is the activation energy (peak minus reactants), not the overall energy given out.","","This is the peak minus the products, which is not the overall energy change.","This is the energy level of the products, not the difference between reactants and products."]}
{"id":"gch-172","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Reaction X: reactants 100 kJ, peak 250 kJ, products 60 kJ. Reaction Y: reactants 100 kJ, peak 180 kJ, products 140 kJ. Which statement is correct?","o":["Y is exothermic because it has the lower activation energy","X is endothermic because its peak is higher than Y's","X is exothermic and has a higher activation energy than Y","X and Y both give out 40 kJ because both change by 40 kJ"],"a":2,"x":"X ends lower than it starts, so it is exothermic. Its activation energy is 150 kJ, higher than Y's 80 kJ. Y ends higher, so it is endothermic. The trap is judging the type by the peak.","w":["Y's products are higher than its reactants, so it is endothermic; activation energy does not decide the type.","The type depends on products versus reactants, not peak height, and X's products are lower so it is exothermic.","","X gives out 40 kJ but Y takes in 40 kJ, so this ignores the direction of the change."]}
{"id":"gch-173","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.3","lv":3,"q":"CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. Bond energies in kJ/mol: C–H 413, O=O 498, C=O 805, O–H 464. What is the overall energy change?","o":["+818 kJ/mol","+110 kJ/mol","−1316 kJ/mol","−818 kJ/mol"],"a":3,"x":"Bonds broken: 4 × 413 + 2 × 498 = 2648. Bonds made: 2 × 805 + 4 × 464 = 3466. Change = 2648 − 3466 = −818 kJ/mol. The trap is missing bonds: CH₄ has four C–H and two H₂O have four O–H.","w":["Sign flipped: bonds made minus bonds broken gives +818, but combustion of methane is exothermic.","Counted only two O–H bonds (one water molecule), so too little energy was released on making bonds.","Used only one O=O bond and forgot the 2 in front of O₂, so too little energy was needed to break bonds.",""]}
{"id":"gch-174","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.3","lv":3,"q":"N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ has an overall energy change of −93 kJ/mol. N≡N is 945 and H–H is 436 kJ/mol. What is the N–H bond energy in kJ/mol?","o":["360","391","782","1173"],"a":1,"x":"Energy to break bonds = 945 + 3 × 436 = 2253. Energy released making bonds = 2253 + 93 = 2346. Two NH₃ have 6 N–H bonds, so 2346 ÷ 6 = 391 kJ/mol. The trap is dividing by the wrong number of bonds.","w":["Sign slip: subtracted 93 instead of adding it, so the energy released making bonds came out too small.","","Divided by 3, the number of N–H bonds in one NH₃, but the equation makes two NH₃ molecules.","Divided by 2, the number of NH₃ molecules, instead of the 6 N–H bonds made."]}
{"id":"gch-175","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"A simple cell is made from two different metals in an electrolyte. Which change would give a bigger voltage?","o":["Use bigger pieces of the same two metals","Use two metals that are further apart in the reactivity series","Use a larger volume of the same electrolyte","Use two electrodes made of the same metal"],"a":1,"x":"The voltage depends on the electrodes and the electrolyte. Metals further apart in the reactivity series give a bigger voltage. Making the pieces or the volume bigger does not change it. The trap is thinking size matters.","w":["Electrode size does not change the voltage; the type of metals does. This is the 'bigger is stronger' slip.","","The amount of electrolyte does not set the voltage; the choice of metals does.","Two identical metals give no voltage, because there is no difference in reactivity to drive the reaction."]}
{"id":"gch-176","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"An alkaline battery stops working and cannot be recharged. What is the reason?","o":["It has no electrolyte inside it to carry charge","Its voltage is too high for it to be reversed","Its reaction makes water that blocks the electrodes","Its reaction is not reversible, so a reactant gets used up"],"a":3,"x":"In a non-rechargeable battery the chemicals react until a reactant is used up, and the voltage drops to zero. A current cannot reverse the reaction. Rechargeable cells are different because their reaction can be reversed.","w":["Every cell needs an electrolyte, including alkaline ones. The cell stops because a reactant is used up.","Voltage size does not decide this. The reaction is simply not reversible by a current.","Water blocking the electrodes is invented. The real reason is that a reactant is used up.",""]}
{"id":"gch-177","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A battery has 6 identical cells connected in series. Its total voltage is 9.0 V. What is the voltage of one cell?","o":["1.5 V","3.0 V","54 V","9.0 V"],"a":0,"x":"Cells in series add their voltages. So one cell is 9.0 ÷ 6 = 1.5 V. The trap is multiplying instead of dividing, or thinking the battery voltage equals one cell.","w":["","This divides 9.0 by 3, not by the 6 cells in the battery.","This multiplies 9.0 by 6 instead of dividing; series voltages add up to the total.","This assumes each cell equals the whole battery, forgetting that series cells add their voltages."]}
{"id":"gch-178","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A hydrogen fuel cell can keep giving voltage for as long as it is used. A rechargeable battery stops and must be plugged in. Why does the fuel cell not stop?","o":["Its reaction is reversed by an electric current","It has a much larger store of chemicals sealed inside","Hydrogen and oxygen are continuously supplied to it","Its electrodes are made of metals that never react"],"a":2,"x":"A fuel cell is supplied with fuel (hydrogen) and oxygen from outside. The reaction continues while they are supplied. A rechargeable battery has a fixed store of chemicals inside. Fuel cells do not need recharging.","w":["Reversing the reaction with a current describes recharging a battery, not how a fuel cell works.","A fuel cell does not rely on a sealed store. The reactants keep arriving from outside.","","The electrodes do not make it endless; the fuel supply does. This confuses the electrodes with the reactants."]}
{"id":"gch-179","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.2","lv":3,"q":"A company says hydrogen fuel cell cars are 'pollution free'. Which fact best challenges this claim?","o":["The car's only exhaust product is water","Most hydrogen is made from methane, which releases carbon dioxide","The fuel cell needs oxygen from the air","The reaction in the fuel cell releases energy"],"a":1,"x":"The car itself only gives out water. But making hydrogen often uses fossil fuels such as methane, which releases CO₂. So the whole process is not pollution free. The trap is judging only the car, not hydrogen production.","w":["This supports the claim, because water at the exhaust is not pollution. It does not challenge it.","","Needing oxygen from the air does not cause pollution, so it does not challenge the claim.","Any fuel cell releases energy as electricity. This does not say anything about pollution."]}
{"id":"gch-180","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A student draws a tangent to a curve of gas volume against time at 30 s. What does the gradient of this tangent show?","o":["The mean rate over the whole reaction","The total volume of gas made by 30 s","The rate of reaction at 30 s","The time when the reaction stops"],"a":2,"x":"A tangent touches the curve at one point. Its gradient is the rate at that exact time. A straight line from the start to the end gives the mean rate instead.","w":["A tangent gives the rate at one moment. The mean rate over the whole reaction needs a line from start to end.","The total volume is read from the vertical axis at 30 s. It is not found from a gradient.","","The reaction stops where the curve goes flat. A tangent gradient does not give a time."]}
{"id":"gch-181","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":1,"q":"4.8 g of magnesium reacts completely with acid in 120 s. What is the mean rate of reaction in g/s?","o":["0.04 g/s","25 g/s","2.4 g/s","576 g/s"],"a":0,"x":"Mean rate = quantity of reactant used ÷ time taken = 4.8 ÷ 120 = 0.04 g/s. Keep the time in seconds so the unit is g/s.","w":["","Divided time by mass (120 ÷ 4.8). Rate is mass ÷ time, not time ÷ mass.","Changed 120 s to 2 minutes then divided. This gives g/min, not g/s.","Multiplied mass by time. Rate needs a division, not a multiplication."]}
{"id":"gch-182","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"On a graph of gas volume against time, a tangent at 40 s passes through (20 s, 10 cm³) and (60 s, 38 cm³). What is the rate of reaction at 40 s?","o":["0.63 cm³/s","1.4 cm³/s","0.47 cm³/s","0.7 cm³/s"],"a":3,"x":"Rate = gradient of tangent = change in volume ÷ change in time = (38 − 10) ÷ (60 − 20) = 28 ÷ 40 = 0.7 cm³/s. Use differences, not single readings.","w":["Divided 38 by 60, using only the end point. You must use the change in both volume and time.","Divided time change by volume change (40 ÷ 28). The gradient is volume ÷ time.","Divided 28 by 60. The time change is 60 − 20 = 40 s, not 60 s.",""]}
{"id":"gch-183","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A flask loses mass as gas escapes. It falls from 80.0 g to 78.8 g in 40 s, then stays at 78.8 g until 100 s. What is the mean rate over the first 100 s?","o":["0.030 g/s","0.012 g/s","0.788 g/s","83 g/s"],"a":1,"x":"Mass lost = 80.0 − 78.8 = 1.2 g. Mean rate over 100 s = 1.2 ÷ 100 = 0.012 g/s. The trap is using 40 s, which ignores the time when the mass stayed constant.","w":["Used 40 s, so this is the mean rate for the first 40 s only, not over 100 s.","","Divided the final mass 78.8 by 100. You need the mass lost, not the mass left.","Divided time by mass lost (100 ÷ 1.2). Rate is mass ÷ time."]}
{"id":"gch-184","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A reaction makes gas at a mean rate of 0.60 cm³/s for the first 40 s. It then makes gas at a mean rate of 0.20 cm³/s for the next 60 s. What is the mean rate over all 100 s?","o":["0.40 cm³/s","0.12 cm³/s","0.36 cm³/s","0.60 cm³/s"],"a":2,"x":"Volume in first 40 s = 0.60 × 40 = 24 cm³. Next 60 s = 0.20 × 60 = 12 cm³. Total 36 cm³ ÷ 100 s = 0.36 cm³/s. Do not average the two rates.","w":["Averaged the two rates, (0.60 + 0.20) ÷ 2. The stages last different times, so this is wrong.","Divided only the second-stage volume (12 cm³) by 100 s, ignoring the gas made in the first stage.","","Used the first rate only, ignoring the slower second stage of the reaction."]}
{"id":"gch-185","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":1,"q":"In collision theory, what is meant by the activation energy of a reaction?","o":["The total energy given out when the reaction is finished","The minimum energy particles need when they collide for a reaction to happen","The average energy of all the particles in the reaction mixture","The energy needed to make the reactants dissolve in water"],"a":1,"x":"Activation energy is the minimum energy colliding particles must have to react. Collisions with less energy than this just bounce apart. It is not the energy released or the average energy.","w":["This confuses activation energy with the energy released to the surroundings in an exothermic reaction.","","Activation energy is a minimum needed for a successful collision, not an average of all particles.","Activation energy is about breaking bonds in colliding particles, not about dissolving."]}
{"id":"gch-186","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Equal masses of marble chips and marble powder are added to the same acid. The powder reacts faster. Why?","o":["The powder has more surface exposed, so acid particles collide with it more often","The powder particles move faster, so they collide with more energy","The powder has a lower activation energy than the chips","The powder contains more marble particles than the chips"],"a":0,"x":"Powder has a much larger surface area for the same mass, so more marble particles are exposed to the acid. Collisions are more frequent. The number of particles and the activation energy do not change.","w":["","Particle speed depends on temperature, not on whether the solid is powdered or in lumps.","Only a catalyst lowers activation energy; breaking a solid into powder does not.","The masses are equal, so the number of marble particles is the same; only the exposed surface is bigger."]}
{"id":"gch-187","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Manganese(IV) oxide is added to hydrogen peroxide solution and the reaction speeds up. Which statement about a catalyst is correct?","o":["It gives the particles more energy so more collisions are successful","It is used up, so its mass is lower at the end","It makes more product than the same reaction without it","It provides a different pathway with a lower activation energy"],"a":3,"x":"A catalyst gives an alternative pathway with lower activation energy, so more collisions are successful. It is not used up and does not change the amount of product. The common trap is thinking it adds energy.","w":["A catalyst does not heat the particles or add energy; it lowers the energy needed.","A catalyst is not used up; it can be recovered unchanged at the end.","The amount of product depends on the reactants used; a catalyst only changes the speed.",""]}
{"id":"gch-188","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Two gases react in a sealed syringe at constant temperature. The pressure is increased by pushing in the plunger. Why does the rate increase?","o":["The particles move faster, so they collide with more energy","The activation energy of the reaction is lowered","The particles are closer together, so collisions happen more often","More particles have energy above the activation energy"],"a":2,"x":"Higher pressure squeezes the same number of gas particles into a smaller volume. They are closer, so they collide more frequently. Temperature is constant, so their energy does not change.","w":["Particle speed depends on temperature, which is constant here.","Only a catalyst lowers activation energy; pressure does not.","","The proportion with enough energy depends on temperature, which has not changed."]}
{"id":"gch-189","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A cube of side 2 cm is cut into eight cubes of side 1 cm. What happens to the total surface area and the surface area to volume ratio?","o":["Area doubles from 24 cm² to 48 cm²; ratio doubles from 3:1 to 6:1","Area stays at 24 cm² because the total volume is the same","Area rises from 24 cm² to 192 cm²; ratio rises from 3:1 to 24:1","Area doubles from 24 cm² to 48 cm²; ratio stays at 3:1"],"a":0,"x":"The 2 cm cube has area 6×4 = 24 cm² and volume 8 cm³, ratio 3:1. Each 1 cm cube has area 6 cm², so eight have 48 cm². Volume is still 8 cm³, so the ratio is 6:1.","w":["","Volume stays the same but the new cut faces add surface area, so the area increases.","This multiplies the original 24 cm² by 8, forgetting each small cube is much smaller.","The volume is still 8 cm³ but the area has doubled, so the ratio must double too."]}
{"id":"gch-190","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Blue hydrated copper(II) sulfate is heated and turns white as water is driven off. Adding water to the white solid turns it blue and the tube gets warm. What does this show?","o":["The forward reaction is exothermic and the reverse reaction is endothermic","Both the forward and reverse reactions release energy to the surroundings","The forward reaction is endothermic and the reverse reaction is exothermic","The reaction is not reversible because the colour changes"],"a":2,"x":"Heating is needed to drive off the water, so the forward reaction is endothermic. The tube warms when water is added, so the reverse reaction is exothermic. The trap is swapping the two directions.","w":["This swaps the directions, because heat must be supplied to drive off the water, so the forward reaction is endothermic.","The two directions of a reversible reaction always have opposite energy changes, so both cannot release energy.","","A colour change does not stop a reaction being reversed, and the solid turns blue again when water is added."]}
{"id":"gch-191","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Ammonium chloride is heated at the bottom of a test tube. White solid appears on the cooler glass higher up. Which statement explains this?","o":["Ammonia and hydrogen chloride react together again to form ammonium chloride","Ammonium chloride only evaporates and condenses, with no chemical reaction","Ammonia gas cools down and turns into a solid on its own","Ammonium chloride is oxidised by the air in the tube"],"a":0,"x":"Heating splits ammonium chloride into ammonia and hydrogen chloride gases. In the cooler part they react to form ammonium chloride again. This is a reversible reaction, not just a change of state.","w":["","This mistakes it for a change of state, but the solid breaks down into two different gases, which is a chemical change.","Ammonia does not turn solid on its own here; it reacts with hydrogen chloride to make the white solid.","Air is not involved, because the reverse reaction is between the two gases made on heating."]}
{"id":"gch-192","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"In a reversible reaction, the forward reaction is endothermic and takes in 80 kJ of energy. What is the energy change for the reverse reaction?","o":["Endothermic, taking in 80 kJ","Exothermic, releasing less than 80 kJ","Neither, because the reverse reaction has no energy change","Exothermic, releasing 80 kJ"],"a":3,"x":"In a reversible reaction, the energy change in one direction is equal in size but opposite in type to the other. The reverse reaction is exothermic and releases exactly 80 kJ, not a smaller amount.","w":["The reverse reaction has the opposite type of energy change, so it cannot also be endothermic.","The same amount of energy is transferred in both directions, so it is exactly 80 kJ, not less.","Every reaction has an energy change; the reverse reaction releases the energy the forward one took in.",""]}
{"id":"gch-194","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A closed container starts with only A. The reaction A ⇌ B reaches dynamic equilibrium. How do the rates change from the start until equilibrium is reached?","o":["The forward rate falls to zero and the reverse rate rises to a maximum","Both rates rise until they become equal","Forward rate falls and reverse rate rises until equal, but not zero","The reverse rate starts equal to the forward rate and both stay the same"],"a":2,"x":"At the start there is only A, so the reverse rate is zero. A is used up, so the forward rate falls; B builds up, so the reverse rate rises. At equilibrium they are equal but not zero.","w":["This ignores the dynamic nature, because the forward reaction keeps going at equilibrium, so its rate never falls to zero.","The forward rate falls as A is used up, so both rates cannot rise.","","The reverse rate starts at zero because there is no B, so the rates are only equal at equilibrium."]}
{"id":"gch-195","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":3,"q":"N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) is exothermic in the forward direction. Which single change moves the position of equilibrium to the right?","o":["Raising the temperature to 600 °C","Lowering the pressure of the gas mixture","Removing the ammonia as a liquid as it forms","Adding more iron catalyst to the reactor"],"a":2,"x":"Removing NH₃ lowers its concentration, so the system makes more NH₃ to oppose the change. A catalyst only speeds up both reactions equally. Heat and low pressure both favour the left.","w":["Raising the temperature favours the endothermic backward reaction, so the position moves left, not right.","Lower pressure favours the side with more gas molecules, which is the left side with 4 mol.","","A catalyst speeds up both reactions equally, so it does not change the position of equilibrium."]}
{"id":"gch-196","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A sealed gas syringe holds brown NO₂ in equilibrium with colourless N₂O₄: 2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g). The plunger is pushed in quickly and held. What is seen?","o":["Darker at first, then slowly paler as the position shifts towards N₂O₄","Paler at first, then slowly darker as the position shifts towards NO₂","Darker at first, then darker still as the position shifts towards NO₂","No change in colour, because pressure does not affect this equilibrium"],"a":0,"x":"Squashing the gas raises the concentration of NO₂ at once, so it looks darker. Higher pressure then favours the side with fewer gas molecules (1 mol N₂O₄, not 2 mol NO₂), so the brown fades.","w":["","Compression raises the NO₂ concentration instantly, so it darkens first, and the shift is towards fewer gas molecules, not more.","Higher pressure shifts towards fewer gas molecules, which is N₂O₄, so the brown colour fades rather than deepening.","Pressure does shift this equilibrium because the sides differ in gas moles: 2 mol NO₂ against 1 mol N₂O₄."]}
{"id":"gch-197","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about crude oil is correct?","o":["It is a single compound made of carbon and hydrogen only","It is a mixture of hydrocarbons, most of which are alkanes","It is a renewable resource that forms again within a few years","It is a compound formed when hydrocarbons bond chemically together"],"a":1,"x":"Crude oil is a mixture of many hydrocarbons, mostly alkanes. The molecules are not chemically joined to each other. It formed from ancient plankton over millions of years, so it is finite.","w":["Crude oil is a mixture of many different molecules, not a single compound.","","Crude oil took millions of years to form, so it is finite, not renewable.","The hydrocarbons in crude oil are not chemically bonded to each other. It is a mixture, not a compound."]}
{"id":"gch-198","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Octane is an alkane with 8 carbon atoms in each molecule. How many hydrogen atoms are in one molecule of octane?","o":["16","20","18","17"],"a":2,"x":"Alkanes follow the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. For n = 8, the hydrogen atoms number 2 × 8 + 2 = 18. Forgetting the '+2' gives 16, which is the formula for an alkene.","w":["This is 2n and forgets the +2. It would fit an alkene such as C₈H₁₆.","This adds 4 instead of 2, so it is 2n + 4.","","This is 2n + 1, which gives an odd number. Alkanes have 2n + 2 hydrogens."]}
{"id":"gch-199","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":2,"q":"In a fractional distillation column the top is cooler than the bottom. Why does a fraction of short-chain hydrocarbons leave near the top?","o":["Its low boiling point means it stays a gas until it reaches the cool top","Its molecules are heavy, so they are carried up the column","Its high boiling point means it condenses first, at the top","Its low viscosity lets it flow up the column as a liquid"],"a":0,"x":"Hot vapours rise up the column. Each fraction condenses where the temperature falls below its boiling point. Short chains have low boiling points, so they only condense near the cool top.","w":["","Heavy, long-chain molecules condense low down. Position depends on boiling point, not on being 'carried up'.","Short chains have low boiling points, not high. Fractions with high boiling points condense near the hot bottom.","The hydrocarbons rise as gases, not as flowing liquids. Viscosity does not decide where they leave."]}
{"id":"gch-200","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A hydrocarbon burns completely in plenty of air. Which statement describes what happens to the hydrocarbon?","o":["Only its carbon is oxidised, and its hydrogen is released as H₂ gas","The hydrocarbon is reduced to form carbon dioxide and water","The oxygen is oxidised and the hydrocarbon is reduced","Both its carbon and hydrogen are oxidised, forming CO₂ and H₂O"],"a":3,"x":"In complete combustion the hydrocarbon gains oxygen. Carbon becomes carbon dioxide and hydrogen becomes water, so both elements are oxidised. A common slip is to say hydrogen is released as a gas.","w":["Hydrogen is also oxidised, forming water. It is not released as H₂ gas.","Gaining oxygen is oxidation, not reduction. The hydrocarbon is oxidised.","The oxygen is the substance that is reduced here. The hydrocarbon gains oxygen, so it is oxidised.",""]}
{"id":"gch-201","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Butane burns completely: 2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O. How many oxygen molecules are needed to burn 4 molecules of butane completely?","o":["13","16","26","52"],"a":2,"x":"The equation shows 2 butane need 13 O₂. For 4 butane, double everything: 26 O₂. Check: 16 CO₂ and 20 H₂O contain 52 oxygen atoms, which is 26 molecules. 52 counts atoms, not molecules.","w":["This is the number for 2 butane molecules. It does not scale up to 4.","This only counts the oxygen needed to make CO₂ and ignores the oxygen in the water.","","This counts oxygen atoms, not O₂ molecules. Each O₂ molecule has 2 atoms."]}
{"id":"gch-202","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.4","lv":1,"q":"Pentene is an alkene with five carbon atoms in each molecule. What is its molecular formula?","o":["C₅H₁₂","C₅H₈","C₅H₁₀","C₅H₂"],"a":2,"x":"Alkenes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ. For n = 5, the formula is C₅H₁₀. C₅H₁₂ is pentane, an alkane (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂).","w":["This is the alkane pentane, from using the alkane formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ instead of the alkene formula.","This has two hydrogens too few; the alkene formula is 2n hydrogens, not 2n − 2.","","This writes the 2 from the formula as the number of hydrogens, instead of 2n."]}
{"id":"gch-203","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A gas made by cracking a hydrocarbon is bubbled through orange bromine water. The bromine water turns colourless. What can you conclude?","o":["At least one product is an alkene","Every product is an alkene","The gas contains no alkanes","The gas is hydrogen"],"a":0,"x":"Bromine water is decolourised by C=C double bonds, so at least one alkene is present. Cracking gives a mixture, so alkanes can be present too. The test does not show that all products are alkenes.","w":["","Cracking makes a mixture; the test only shows an alkene is present, not that every product is one.","Alkanes do not react with bromine water, so the test cannot show whether they are present or absent.","Hydrogen does not decolourise bromine water; the colour change is caused by a C=C double bond."]}
{"id":"gch-204","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Decane is cracked: C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + X. What is the formula of X?","o":["C₂H₆","C₂H₂","C₄H₈","C₂H₄"],"a":3,"x":"Atoms must balance. Carbon: 10 − 8 = 2. Hydrogen: 22 − 18 = 4. So X is C₂H₄, which is ethene, an alkene. It is a trap to expect an alkane product.","w":["This gives 24 hydrogens in total, not 22; it comes from wrongly thinking both products must be alkanes.","This gives only 20 hydrogens in total, so the equation does not balance.","This gives 12 carbons in total, not 10, so the carbon atoms do not balance.",""]}
{"id":"gch-205","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.4","lv":1,"q":"Why do oil companies crack long-chain hydrocarbons from crude oil?","o":["Long chains have boiling points too low to be useful","There is more demand for short-chain hydrocarbons than crude oil supplies","Cracking separates crude oil into different fractions","Cracking joins small molecules into useful polymers"],"a":1,"x":"Crude oil contains more long-chain hydrocarbons than are needed. Cracking turns them into shorter alkanes for fuels and into alkenes for making polymers. Separating fractions is fractional distillation, not cracking.","w":["Long chains have high boiling points, not low, and this is not the reason for cracking.","","This mixes up cracking with fractional distillation, which separates crude oil by boiling point.","Cracking breaks molecules down; polymers are made by a different reaction, polymerisation."]}
{"id":"gch-206","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.4","lv":3,"q":"C₁₇H₃₆ is cracked: C₁₇H₃₆ → C₈H₁₈ + C₃H₆ + x C₂H₄. What is the value of x?","o":["2","6","3","12"],"a":2,"x":"Carbon: 17 − 8 − 3 = 6 carbons left. Each C₂H₄ has 2 carbons, so x = 3. Check hydrogen: 18 + 6 + 12 = 36. The trap is forgetting to divide by the atoms in one molecule.","w":["This gives only 15 carbons in total, not 17, so the carbon atoms do not balance.","This is the number of carbon atoms left over; it must be divided by 2 carbons in each C₂H₄.","","This is the number of hydrogen atoms left over; it must be divided by 4 hydrogens in each C₂H₄."]}
{"id":"gch-207","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"An alkene has 6 carbon atoms in each molecule. How many hydrogen atoms does one molecule have?","o":["14","12","6","10"],"a":1,"x":"Alkenes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ. For n = 6 the formula is C₆H₁₂, so there are 12 hydrogen atoms. The trap is using the alkane formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","w":["14 is the number for an alkane, C₆H₁₄, which uses the formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ instead of CₙH₂ₙ.","","6 wrongly assumes one hydrogen per carbon atom. Alkenes have twice as many hydrogens as carbons.","10 is two fewer than 2n, so it does not fit CₙH₂ₙ. It is not the formula of any alkene with 6 carbons."]}
{"id":"gch-208","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Propene, C₃H₆, reacts with bromine, Br₂. What is the formula of the only product?","o":["C₃H₇Br","C₃H₆Br","C₃H₄Br₂","C₃H₆Br₂"],"a":3,"x":"This is an addition reaction. A whole Br₂ molecule adds across the C=C bond, with one Br on each carbon. Nothing is lost, so the product is C₃H₆Br₂. The trap is thinking hydrogen atoms get replaced.","w":["C₃H₇Br has only one bromine and an extra hydrogen. It comes from adding HBr, not Br₂.","C₃H₆Br has only one bromine atom. Br₂ adds as two atoms, one to each carbon of the double bond.","C₃H₄Br₂ loses two hydrogens, as if bromine replaced them. Alkenes add across the double bond, so no hydrogen is lost.",""]}
{"id":"gch-209","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Ethene gas is shaken with orange bromine water. What happens, and why?","o":["It turns colourless because bromine adds across the C=C bond","It stays orange because ethene is saturated","It turns darker orange because more bromine is added to the ethene","It turns colourless because ethene neutralises the bromine"],"a":0,"x":"Ethene is unsaturated. Bromine adds across the C=C double bond and makes a colourless product, so the orange colour disappears. The trap is saying alkenes are saturated, or calling it neutralisation.","w":["","Ethene is unsaturated, not saturated. It has a C=C bond, so it does react and the colour goes.","Adding bromine to the alkene uses up the coloured bromine, so the mixture loses colour. It does not get darker.","Neutralisation is acid with alkali. This is an addition reaction, not an acid–base reaction."]}
{"id":"gch-210","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":3,"q":"An alkene has a relative formula mass (Mr) of 70. How many carbon atoms are in one molecule? (Ar: C = 12, H = 1)","o":["4","6","5","10"],"a":2,"x":"Alkenes are CₙH₂ₙ, so Mr = 12n + 2n = 14n. Then 14n = 70, so n = 5 (C₅H₁₀). The trap is ignoring hydrogen, or counting hydrogen atoms instead of carbon atoms.","w":["An alkene with 4 carbons is C₄H₈, with Mr 4 × 14 = 56, not 70.","6 comes from dividing 70 by 12 and ignoring the hydrogen atoms in the molecule.","","10 is the number of hydrogen atoms in C₅H₁₀, not the number of carbon atoms."]}
{"id":"gch-211","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"But-1-ene, C₄H₈, reacts with hydrogen using a nickel catalyst. Which row gives the formula of the product and whether it is saturated?","o":["C₄H₆, unsaturated","C₄H₁₀, saturated","C₄H₁₀, unsaturated","C₄H₆, saturated"],"a":1,"x":"Hydrogen adds across the C=C bond, turning it into a C–C single bond. C₄H₈ + H₂ → C₄H₁₀ (butane). With only single bonds, the product is saturated. The trap is thinking hydrogen is removed.","w":["C₄H₆ has two fewer hydrogens. Hydrogen is added to the alkene, not taken away.","","The formula is right, but butane has no C=C bond left. Its bonds are all single, so it is saturated.","C₄H₆ is wrong because hydrogen is added, not removed. It would also still have a multiple bond."]}
{"id":"gch-212","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A small piece of sodium is added to ethanol. What is seen, and which gas is given off?","o":["Fizzing; carbon dioxide gas is given off","Fizzing; hydrogen gas is given off","No change, because ethanol is a neutral compound","Fizzing; oxygen gas is given off"],"a":1,"x":"Alcohols react with sodium to make hydrogen gas, which causes the fizzing. Ethanol is neutral in water, but this does not stop it reacting with sodium. Carbon dioxide comes from acids reacting with carbonates.","w":["Carbon dioxide is made when acids react with carbonates. Sodium with an alcohol makes hydrogen.","","Ethanol is neutral, but it still reacts with sodium through its –OH group. Neutral does not mean unreactive.","Oxygen is not made. The –OH hydrogen is released as hydrogen gas."]}
{"id":"gch-213","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Ethanol is made by fermenting sugar solution. Which set of conditions is used?","o":["Yeast, about 30–40 °C, plenty of oxygen from the air","Yeast, about 100 °C, no oxygen","Yeast, about 0 °C, no oxygen","Yeast, about 30–40 °C, no oxygen"],"a":3,"x":"Fermentation uses yeast, a warm temperature of about 30–40 °C, and no oxygen (anaerobic). Too hot kills the enzymes in yeast. Too cold is far too slow. Oxygen can oxidise the ethanol.","w":["Plenty of oxygen is wrong. Fermentation is anaerobic, and air can turn ethanol into ethanoic acid.","100 °C would denature the enzymes in the yeast, so fermentation would stop.","At 0 °C the enzymes work far too slowly. Fermentation needs a warm temperature.",""]}
{"id":"gch-214","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A bottle of wine is left open for several weeks. It begins to taste of vinegar. Which statement explains this?","o":["Microbes and oxygen from the air oxidised the ethanol to ethanoic acid","Oxygen reduced the ethanol to ethanoic acid","The ethanol was dehydrated to make ethene gas","Yeast fermented the remaining sugar into ethanoic acid"],"a":0,"x":"Microbes and oxygen from the air oxidise ethanol to ethanoic acid, which gives the sour vinegar taste. Gaining oxygen is oxidation, not reduction. Fermentation of sugar makes ethanol, not ethanoic acid.","w":["","Adding oxygen is oxidation, not reduction. This is the slip of mixing up the two terms.","Ethene is not formed by leaving wine open. The sour taste comes from an acid.","Fermentation of sugar makes ethanol and carbon dioxide. Ethanoic acid forms later by oxidation."]}
{"id":"gch-215","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Ethanoic acid solution is added to sodium carbonate. Which are the products of the reaction?","o":["Sodium ethanoate and hydrogen","Sodium ethanoate and water only","Sodium ethanoate, water and carbon dioxide","Ethyl ethanoate and water"],"a":2,"x":"Carboxylic acids react with carbonates to make a salt, water and carbon dioxide. Here the salt is sodium ethanoate. Hydrogen is made when acids react with metals, not carbonates. This is the trap.","w":["Hydrogen is made when an acid reacts with a metal. A carbonate gives carbon dioxide instead.","This leaves out carbon dioxide. Acids react with carbonates to give a salt, water and carbon dioxide.","","Ethyl ethanoate is an ester made from an acid and an alcohol. No alcohol is present here."]}
{"id":"gch-216","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":3,"q":"0.1 mol/dm³ ethanoic acid has pH 3. 0.1 mol/dm³ hydrochloric acid has pH 1. Which statement best explains the difference?","o":["Ethanoic acid has no H⁺ ions because its hydrogen atoms are covalently bonded","Ethanoic acid is a weak acid, so only some molecules ionise and fewer H⁺ ions form","Ethanoic acid is a weak acid, so it ionises completely but more slowly","Ethanoic acid is more dilute because its molecules have more carbon atoms"],"a":1,"x":"Ethanoic acid is a weak acid. Only a small fraction of its molecules ionise in water, so there are fewer H⁺ ions and the pH is higher. The two solutions have the same concentration. Weak is not the same as dilute.","w":["Ethanoic acid does form some H⁺ ions when it ionises. It is acidic, so it cannot have none.","","A weak acid ionises only partly, not completely. Slow speed is not what makes the pH higher.","Both solutions are 0.1 mol/dm³, so they are equally concentrated. Weak and dilute mean different things."]}
{"id":"gch-217","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Ethene molecules join together to make poly(ethene). What happens to the C=C double bond in each ethene molecule?","o":["It breaks and forms ionic bonds between the molecules","It opens up and becomes a single C–C bond","It stays as a double bond in the polymer chain","It becomes a triple bond between the carbon atoms"],"a":1,"x":"In addition polymerisation each C=C opens out into a single C–C bond. The freed bonds link to the next monomer. The trap is thinking the double bond stays in the polymer.","w":["Polymer chains are held by covalent bonds, not ionic ones; this confuses covalent and ionic bonding.","","Poly(ethene) is saturated with no C=C bonds; the double bond is used up when the monomers join.","The double bond opens to a single bond; it does not gain shared electrons to become a triple bond."]}
{"id":"gch-218","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is the correct name for the addition polymer made from propene?","o":["Poly(propene)","Poly(propane)","Poly(ethene)","Poly(methane)"],"a":0,"x":"Addition polymers are named poly(monomer name). The monomer is propene, so the polymer is poly(propene). The trap is naming it after the saturated alkane because the polymer has no double bonds.","w":["","The polymer is named after the monomer, propene, not after the saturated alkane propane.","Poly(ethene) is made from ethene, which has two carbon atoms, not three.","Methane has no C=C bond and is not the monomer; the name must come from propene."]}
{"id":"gch-219","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Propene is CH₃–CH=CH₂. Which one shows the repeat unit of poly(propene)?","o":["–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–","–CH₂=CH(CH₃)–","–CH₂–CH₂–","–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–"],"a":3,"x":"The C=C opens to a single bond. The two carbons of the double bond stay in the chain. The CH₃ group hangs off as a side group. Moving CH₃ into the chain, or keeping the double bond, are common slips.","w":["This puts the methyl carbon into the backbone; in the repeat unit CH₃ is a side group.","The double bond should have opened up; a repeat unit has only single bonds.","This is the repeat unit of poly(ethene); the CH₃ side group of propene is missing.",""]}
{"id":"gch-220","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":2,"q":"500 ethene molecules (C₂H₄) join to make one poly(ethene) chain. Ignoring the ends of the chain, how many hydrogen atoms are in it?","o":["500","1000","2000","4000"],"a":2,"x":"No atoms are lost in addition polymerisation. Each ethene has 4 hydrogen atoms, so 500 × 4 = 2000. The trap is counting carbon atoms (1000) or only one hydrogen per monomer.","w":["This counts one hydrogen per monomer; each ethene molecule has four hydrogen atoms.","This is the number of carbon atoms (500 × 2), not hydrogen atoms.","","This doubles the real number; atoms are not made, so the total H must equal 500 × 4."]}
{"id":"gch-221","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A section of poly(propene) is made from 4 propene molecules, CH₃–CH=CH₂. How many carbon atoms are in the main chain, and how many are in CH₃ side groups?","o":["12 in the main chain, 0 in side groups","8 in the main chain, 4 in side groups","4 in the main chain, 8 in side groups","6 in the main chain, 6 in side groups"],"a":1,"x":"Each propene gives 2 carbons in the chain (the old C=C carbons) and 1 carbon in a CH₃ side group. For 4 molecules that is 8 in the chain and 4 in side groups. Putting all 12 in the chain is the trap.","w":["The total of 12 is right, but the CH₃ carbon stays as a side group, not in the chain.","","This swaps the numbers; each monomer gives two chain carbons and only one side-group carbon.","Each monomer gives 2 chain carbons and 1 side-group carbon, so the split is 8 and 4, not 6 and 6."]}
{"id":"gch-223","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Glycine is NH₂CH₂COOH (Mr = 75). Two glycine molecules join by condensation to make a dipeptide and one small molecule. What is the Mr of the dipeptide? (H₂O = 18)","o":["132","150","114","93"],"a":0,"x":"Two glycine molecules have a total Mr of 150. Condensation loses one water molecule (18), so the dipeptide has Mr 150 − 18 = 132. The trap is forgetting to subtract the small molecule.","w":["","Added the two Mr values but forgot that a water molecule is lost in condensation.","Subtracted two waters (36), but only one link forms so only one water is lost.","Added water to one glycine (75 + 18), but water is lost, and both molecules join."]}
{"id":"gch-224","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.2","lv":3,"q":"A molecule has one –OH group and one –COOH group, one at each end. Which statement about it making a polymer is correct?","o":["It cannot polymerise alone, because two different monomers are always needed","It makes an addition polymer, because there are no C=C bonds in it","It cannot polymerise, because a polyester needs two –OH groups","It can polymerise alone, forming a polyester and losing water at each link"],"a":3,"x":"Each molecule has two different reactive groups. The –OH of one reacts with the –COOH of the next, forming an ester link and losing water. One monomer type can be enough.","w":["Two monomers are not always needed; one monomer with two different functional groups can join to itself.","Addition polymers need C=C bonds and lose nothing; this one forms ester links and loses water.","Each ester link needs only one –OH and one –COOH, and these molecules supply both.",""]}
{"id":"gch-225","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes the structure of most DNA molecules?","o":["One long polymer chain in a straight line","Two polymer chains twisted into a double helix","Three polymer chains twisted together","Many short chains joined in a flat sheet"],"a":1,"x":"Most DNA molecules are two polymer chains twisted together to form a double helix. A common slip is to think DNA is a single chain, just because it is called a polymer.","w":["DNA has two chains, not one; a single straight chain confuses DNA with a simple polymer such as poly(ethene).","","DNA has two chains, not three; a double helix means two strands twisted together.","DNA is a twisted double helix, not a flat sheet of many short chains."]}
{"id":"gch-226","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which of these natural polymers is made from sugar monomers?","o":["Protein","DNA","Cellulose","Poly(ethene)"],"a":2,"x":"Cellulose, like starch, is a polymer made of sugar units. Proteins are made from amino acids and DNA from nucleotides. Poly(ethene) is a man-made polymer, not a natural one.","w":["Proteins are made from amino acids, not sugars.","DNA is made from four different nucleotides, not sugar monomers.","","Poly(ethene) is made from ethene, and it is a man-made polymer, not a natural one."]}
{"id":"gch-227","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":1,"q":"What is the job of DNA in living organisms and viruses?","o":["It encodes the genetic instructions for development and functioning","It stores energy as a long chain of sugar units","It forms strong fibres that strengthen plant cell walls","It is the main store of fat used for insulation"],"a":0,"x":"DNA is a large molecule that encodes the genetic instructions for the development and functioning of living organisms and viruses. Do not confuse it with starch (energy store) or cellulose (cell walls).","w":["","Storing energy as sugar chains is the job of starch, not DNA.","Strengthening plant cell walls is the job of cellulose, not DNA.","Fats are not polymers like DNA, and DNA does not store fat."]}
{"id":"gch-228","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Which row matches each natural polymer with its correct monomer?","o":["DNA: amino acids; protein: nucleotides; starch: sugars","DNA: nucleotides; protein: sugars; starch: amino acids","DNA: sugars; protein: amino acids; starch: nucleotides","DNA: nucleotides; protein: amino acids; starch: sugars"],"a":3,"x":"DNA is made of nucleotides, proteins of amino acids and starch of sugar units. The common error is swapping the monomers between polymers.","w":["DNA and protein monomers are swapped here; DNA is made from nucleotides and protein from amino acids.","Protein and starch monomers are swapped; proteins need amino acids and starch is made from sugars.","DNA and starch monomers are swapped; DNA is made of nucleotides and starch of sugars.",""]}
{"id":"gch-229","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about DNA is correct?","o":["It is made from one type of repeating monomer, like poly(ethene)","It is made from four different nucleotides joined in a long chain","It is made by joining monomers that each contain a C=C double bond","It is a small molecule with a relative formula mass of about 100"],"a":1,"x":"DNA is a polymer built from four different nucleotides. Unlike poly(ethene), it does not have one repeating monomer, and its monomers are not alkenes.","w":["Poly(ethene) has one monomer, but DNA has four different nucleotides; this wrongly treats all polymers as one repeating unit.","","C=C bonds are in addition polymer monomers such as ethene; nucleotides are not alkenes and DNA is not an addition polymer.","DNA is a very large molecule with a huge relative formula mass; thinking it is small confuses it with simple molecules."]}
{"id":"gch-230","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A carton says \"pure orange juice\". Why is this NOT pure in the chemistry sense?","o":["Pure substances can only be solids, and juice is a liquid","It contains many different substances mixed together","It contains water, and water is always an impurity","It was made in a factory, not in a laboratory"],"a":1,"x":"In chemistry, a pure substance is a single element or compound. Juice is a mixture of water, sugars, acids and more. Everyday \"pure\" often just means nothing has been added.","w":["Pure substances can be solids, liquids or gases, so state is not the issue.","","Water is a compound and can be pure itself; it is just one of several substances in juice.","Where something is made does not decide purity; only what it contains does."]}
{"id":"gch-231","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What is a formulation?","o":["A mixture of measured amounts of components, designed as a useful product","A single compound with a fixed chemical formula","A mixture made by combining substances in any amounts","A pure element with a sharp melting point"],"a":0,"x":"A formulation is a designed mixture. Its components are measured in exact quantities so the product has the properties needed. Don't confuse \"formulation\" with a chemical formula.","w":["","This confuses formulation with a chemical formula; a formulation is a mixture, not a single compound.","Formulations need carefully measured amounts, not just any amounts.","A pure element is not a mixture, so it cannot be a formulation."]}
{"id":"gch-232","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Four solids are heated and watched as they melt. Which one is most likely to be a pure substance?","o":["It starts to melt at 61 °C and finishes melting at 68 °C","It starts to melt at 90 °C and finishes melting at 97 °C","It softens slowly over a wide temperature range of about 20 °C","It stays at exactly 80 °C for the whole time it is melting"],"a":3,"x":"A pure substance melts at one fixed temperature. Mixtures and impure samples melt over a range. The trap is thinking a higher melting temperature means purer; it is the sharpness that matters.","w":["A 7 °C melting range shows a mixture or impure sample, not a pure substance.","A higher temperature does not mean purer; this still melts over a range, so it is impure.","Slow softening over a wide range is typical of a mixture, not a pure substance.",""]}
{"id":"gch-233","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A company makes a fertiliser by mixing three compounds. Why must it measure the amounts of each compound carefully?","o":["So that the mixture becomes a single pure compound","So that the three compounds react to make a new substance","So that the fertiliser has the right properties for helping plants grow","So that the fertiliser melts at one sharp temperature"],"a":2,"x":"A fertiliser is a formulation. The amounts of each component are controlled so the product has the properties needed, such as the right balance of nutrients. It stays a mixture; it is not made pure.","w":["Mixing does not make a pure compound; a formulation stays a mixture.","The compounds are simply mixed, not reacted, so no new substance forms.","","A formulation is a mixture, so it would not have a sharp melting point anyway."]}
{"id":"gch-234","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A student finds a sample melts at one sharp temperature. She says, \"So it must be a pure compound, not an element.\" What is wrong with her conclusion?","o":["A sharp melting point shows it is pure, but pure elements melt sharply too","Pure substances always melt over a range, so it is impure","Only mixtures have melting points, so it cannot be pure","Compounds always melt at lower temperatures than elements"],"a":0,"x":"A sharp melting point suggests a pure substance. Pure elements and pure compounds both melt sharply, so this test cannot tell which one it is. Trap: treating \"pure\" as meaning \"compound\".","w":["","Pure substances melt at a sharp temperature, and a range suggests impurity.","All substances have melting points, including pure ones; mixtures melt over a range.","There is no such rule; some elements melt higher than compounds and some lower."]}
{"id":"gch-235","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":1,"q":"In a paper chromatography practical, the start line is drawn in pencil, not ink. Why is pencil used?","o":["Pencil marks are thinner, so the spots are easier to see","Pencil does not dissolve in the solvent, so it will not travel up the paper","Pencil dissolves in the solvent and helps the substances separate","Pencil stops the solvent from rising too quickly"],"a":1,"x":"Pencil (graphite) is insoluble in the solvent, so the line stays put and does not make its own spots. Ink contains dyes that would dissolve, run up the paper and mix with the sample.","w":["Thickness of the line is not the reason; the real issue is whether the line material dissolves.","","Wrong: if the line dissolved it would add extra coloured spots and spoil the result.","The line has no effect on how fast the solvent rises; this is a made-up reason."]}
{"id":"gch-236","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":1,"q":"In paper chromatography, which part of the apparatus is the mobile phase?","o":["The chromatography paper","The pencil start line","The spot of sample on the paper","The solvent that moves up the paper"],"a":3,"x":"The mobile phase is the solvent, which moves up the paper and carries the substances with it. The paper is the stationary phase. Do not mix the two up.","w":["The paper stays still, so it is the stationary phase, not the mobile phase.","The line is just a marker for the start; it is not a phase.","The sample is what is being separated; it is not the mobile phase.",""]}
{"id":"gch-237","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A substance has an Rf value of 0.45 in a solvent. The solvent front moves 12.0 cm. How far does the substance move?","o":["26.7 cm","11.55 cm","5.4 cm","0.0375 cm"],"a":2,"x":"Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent. So distance = 0.45 × 12.0 = 5.4 cm. The trap is dividing or subtracting instead of rearranging correctly.","w":["Divided 12.0 by 0.45; you must multiply Rf by the solvent distance.","Subtracted 0.45 from 12.0; Rf is a ratio, so you multiply.","","Divided 0.45 by 12.0, which turns the formula upside down."]}
{"id":"gch-238","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Known dyes have Rf values: E102 = 0.30, E110 = 0.55, E124 = 0.80. A sweet colouring gives two spots at 2.4 cm and 4.4 cm. The solvent front is at 8.0 cm. Which dyes are in it?","o":["E102 and E110 only","E110 and E124 only","E102 and E124 only","E102, E110 and E124"],"a":0,"x":"Rf values are 2.4 ÷ 8.0 = 0.30 and 4.4 ÷ 8.0 = 0.55. These match E102 and E110. There are only two spots, so E124 is not present.","w":["","The 0.30 spot does not match E124 (0.80); you may have used the spot distances without dividing.","The 4.4 cm spot gives Rf 0.55, which is E110, not E124.","Only two spots appear, so only two dyes are present; do not assume all three."]}
{"id":"gch-239","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":3,"q":"A dye gives one spot in water (Rf 0.60) but two spots in ethanol. What is the correct conclusion?","o":["The dye is pure, because it gave a single spot in water","The dye is a mixture, because a pure substance gives one spot in every solvent","The ethanol must be contaminated, because Rf values never change","The dye is pure, but ethanol breaks it down into two substances"],"a":1,"x":"A pure substance gives a single spot in any solvent. Two spots in ethanol show a mixture. In water the parts happened to travel together. One solvent alone cannot prove purity.","w":["One spot in one solvent is not enough; the parts could just have the same Rf in water.","","Rf values do change between solvents, so this is not evidence of contamination.","Chromatography only separates; it does not break substances down into new ones."]}
{"id":"gch-240","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.2","lv":1,"q":"A student collects a colourless gas in a test tube. Which test shows the gas is hydrogen?","o":["Hold a glowing splint in the gas and it relights","Hold a lit splint at the mouth of the tube and it gives a squeaky pop","Shake the gas with limewater and the limewater turns cloudy","Hold damp litmus paper in the gas and it turns white"],"a":1,"x":"Hydrogen burns rapidly with a squeaky pop when a lit splint is held at the mouth of the tube. A glowing splint relighting is the test for oxygen, a common mix-up.","w":["This is the test for oxygen, not hydrogen; mixing up the glowing splint and lit splint tests is the slip.","","This is the test for carbon dioxide, not hydrogen; limewater does not react with hydrogen.","This is the test for chlorine, not hydrogen; hydrogen does not bleach litmus paper."]}
{"id":"gch-241","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.2","lv":1,"q":"Carbon dioxide is bubbled through limewater. What do you see?","o":["The limewater turns bright pink","The limewater fizzes and a squeaky pop is heard","The limewater turns blue","The limewater turns cloudy"],"a":3,"x":"Carbon dioxide reacts with limewater to make a white solid, calcium carbonate. This makes the limewater look cloudy. Limewater does not change colour, so a colour change is wrong.","w":["Limewater does not turn pink; that confuses it with an indicator such as phenolphthalein.","A squeaky pop is the test for hydrogen; it is not seen with limewater and carbon dioxide.","Limewater does not turn blue; that confuses it with the colour change of an indicator or blue litmus.",""]}
{"id":"gch-242","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid. A lit splint held at the mouth of the test tube gives a squeaky pop. Which gas is present, and what is made in the pop?","o":["Hydrogen; water","Hydrogen; carbon dioxide","Oxygen; water","Carbon dioxide; water"],"a":0,"x":"A squeaky pop shows hydrogen. The hydrogen burns quickly with oxygen in the air to make water. The trap is thinking that the pop shows oxygen or that carbon dioxide is made.","w":["","Hydrogen contains no carbon, so burning it cannot make carbon dioxide; the slip is confusing it with burning a carbon compound.","Oxygen relights a glowing splint; it does not give a squeaky pop. Confusing the two tests is the slip.","Carbon dioxide puts out a lit splint and turns limewater cloudy; it does not give a squeaky pop."]}
{"id":"gch-243","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.2","lv":2,"q":"Litmus paper is used to test for chlorine. Why must the litmus paper be damp?","o":["Dry litmus paper reacts with oxygen in the air","Water makes chlorine gas less poisonous","Chlorine dissolves in the water and the solution bleaches the litmus","Water stops the litmus paper from burning"],"a":2,"x":"Chlorine dissolves in the water on the paper. The solution bleaches the dye, so the paper turns white. Dry chlorine gas does not bleach dry litmus paper, so the paper must be damp.","w":["Oxygen in the air is not the issue; the slip is inventing a reaction with air rather than chlorine and water.","Damp paper does not make chlorine safer; chlorine is still poisonous. The reason is about the bleaching reaction.","","Litmus paper is not at risk of burning in this test; the slip is mixing it up with the splint tests."]}
{"id":"gch-244","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.2","lv":3,"q":"Sodium chloride solution is electrolysed. The gas at the positive electrode bleaches damp litmus paper. The gas at the negative electrode gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint. Which row is correct?","o":["Positive: hydrogen; negative: chlorine","Positive: chlorine; negative: hydrogen","Positive: oxygen; negative: hydrogen","Positive: chlorine; negative: oxygen"],"a":1,"x":"Bleaching damp litmus shows chlorine at the positive electrode. A squeaky pop shows hydrogen at the negative electrode. The trap is swapping the gases or matching the tests to the wrong gas.","w":["The gases are swapped; bleaching shows chlorine and the squeaky pop shows hydrogen, so the electrodes are the wrong way round.","","Oxygen does not bleach damp litmus paper; only chlorine does. The slip is matching the wrong gas to the bleaching test.","Oxygen would relight a glowing splint. A squeaky pop shows hydrogen, so oxygen is the wrong gas here."]}
{"id":"gch-245","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.1","lv":1,"q":"A student adds dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution to a colourless solution. A cream precipitate forms. Which ion is in the solution?","o":["Chloride, Cl⁻","Bromide, Br⁻","Iodide, I⁻","Carbonate, CO₃²⁻"],"a":1,"x":"With silver nitrate, bromide ions give a cream precipitate. Chloride gives white and iodide gives yellow. Carbonate would fizz with the nitric acid and does not give a cream precipitate.","w":["Chloride gives a white precipitate with silver nitrate, so this mixes up the halide colours.","","Iodide gives a yellow precipitate with silver nitrate, so this mixes up the halide colours.","Carbonate is tested by fizzing with acid, so this confuses the carbonate test with the halide test."]}
{"id":"gch-246","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Sodium hydroxide solution is added to a solution of a metal compound. A brown precipitate forms. Which metal ion is present?","o":["Iron(II), Fe²⁺","Copper(II), Cu²⁺","Iron(III), Fe³⁺","Aluminium, Al³⁺"],"a":2,"x":"Iron(III) hydroxide is a brown precipitate. Iron(II) gives a green precipitate. The trap is mixing up the two iron ions because both contain iron.","w":["Iron(II) hydroxide is green, so this mixes up the two iron ions.","Copper(II) hydroxide is blue, so this confuses the coloured hydroxide precipitates.","","Aluminium hydroxide is white and dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide, so it cannot be brown."]}
{"id":"gch-247","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Sodium hydroxide solution is added to a colourless solution. A white precipitate forms. It does not dissolve when excess sodium hydroxide is added. Which ions could be present?","o":["Aluminium ions only","Copper(II) or iron(II) ions","Sodium ions only","Calcium or magnesium ions"],"a":3,"x":"Calcium and magnesium hydroxides are white and stay when excess alkali is added. Aluminium hydroxide is also white but dissolves in excess. The trap is thinking every white precipitate means aluminium.","w":["Aluminium hydroxide dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide, but here the white precipitate stays.","Copper(II) hydroxide is blue and iron(II) hydroxide is green, so neither gives a white precipitate.","Sodium ions form no precipitate with sodium hydroxide, so this wrongly links sodium to a solid forming.",""]}
{"id":"gch-248","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A sample contains a mixture of two different metal ions. Why might a flame test fail to identify both metal ions?","o":["One flame colour can mask the other colour","Metal ions in a mixture give no flame colour","The two ions react to form a precipitate","Flame tests only work on pure solid samples"],"a":0,"x":"In a mixture, one strong flame colour can hide (mask) another, so a flame test may show only one metal. The trap is thinking mixtures give no colour at all.","w":["","Mixtures still give a flame colour; the problem is that one colour can hide another.","Ions do not need to react for a flame test; the issue is one colour masking another.","Flame tests work on solids and solutions; the problem with mixtures is colours masking each other."]}
{"id":"gch-249","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.1","lv":3,"q":"Solution Z gives an orange-red flame. Sodium hydroxide gives a white precipitate that stays in excess. Barium chloride with acid gives no change. Silver nitrate with nitric acid gives a white precipitate. What is Z?","o":["Calcium sulfate","Magnesium chloride","Calcium chloride","Calcium bromide"],"a":2,"x":"Orange-red flame and a white precipitate that stays in excess show Ca²⁺. No change with barium chloride rules out sulfate. A white precipitate with silver nitrate shows chloride. The trap is magnesium, which gives no flame colour.","w":["A sulfate would give a white precipitate with barium chloride and acid, but there was no change.","Magnesium gives no flame colour, so the orange-red flame shows calcium, not magnesium.","","Bromide gives a cream precipitate with silver nitrate, so the white precipitate shows chloride instead."]}
{"id":"gch-250","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.6","lv":1,"q":"In flame emission spectroscopy, what is compared to identify a metal ion in a sample?","o":["The colour of the flame, matched to a chart by eye","The line spectrum of the sample, matched to known reference spectra","The mass of solid left after the sample is heated","The colour of a precipitate made with sodium hydroxide"],"a":1,"x":"The sample is put in a flame and the light goes through a spectroscope. This gives a line spectrum, which is compared with known spectra. Judging flame colour by eye is the simple flame test, not the instrument.","w":["This describes a simple flame test, which depends on the eye, not on an instrument and a spectrum.","","Heating and weighing is not part of flame emission spectroscopy, so no mass is compared.","This is a chemical test for some metal ions, not an instrumental method."]}
{"id":"gch-251","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.6","lv":2,"q":"A water sample contains sodium ions and potassium ions. Why is flame emission spectroscopy better than a simple flame test for this sample?","o":["It does not need the sample to be put in a flame","It gives one flame colour that is easier to see by eye","It makes the two ions react to form a precipitate","It gives separate lines for each ion, so both are identified"],"a":3,"x":"In a mixture, flame colours can mix or hide each other, so sodium's yellow can mask other ions. The spectroscope splits the light into lines for each ion, so both can be identified.","w":["The sample is still put in a flame; the light it gives out is then passed through a spectroscope.","A mixture gives mixed colours that are harder to judge. One clear colour is the problem, not the benefit.","Precipitates are used in chemical tests with sodium hydroxide, not in flame emission spectroscopy.",""]}
{"id":"gch-252","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.6","lv":2,"q":"Two solutions each contain only copper ions. Flame emission spectra show lines in the same positions, but the lines for solution X are more intense than for Y. What does this show?","o":["X has a higher concentration of copper ions than Y","X contains a different metal ion from Y","X contains more types of metal ion than Y","X is more acidic than Y"],"a":0,"x":"Line positions show which ion is present. Line intensity shows how much of it there is. Same positions means the same ion, so the brighter lines mean X is more concentrated.","w":["","A different ion would give lines in different positions. The positions here are the same.","More types of ion would add extra lines in new positions, not make the same lines brighter.","Flame emission spectroscopy does not measure acidity. Intensity is linked to concentration, not pH."]}
{"id":"gch-253","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.6","lv":3,"q":"Reference line spectra: ion P has lines at 450 nm and 590 nm. Ion Q has lines at 590 nm and 670 nm. Ion R has lines at 460 nm and 610 nm. A sample shows lines at 450, 590 and 670 nm. Which ions are present?","o":["P only","Q only","P and Q","P, Q and R"],"a":2,"x":"The 450 nm line shows P and the 670 nm line shows Q. The 590 nm line is shared, so it does not tell them apart. No lines at 460 or 610 nm, so R is absent.","w":["P explains 450 and 590 nm, but nothing explains the 670 nm line. The extra line shows a second ion.","Q explains 590 and 670 nm, but nothing explains the 450 nm line. The extra line shows a second ion.","","R would need lines at 460 nm and 610 nm. Neither is in the sample, so R is not present."]}
{"id":"gch-254","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.6","lv":1,"q":"Which pair of statements about a flame emission spectrum of a metal ion is correct?","o":["Line positions show concentration; line intensity shows which ion","Line positions show which ion; line intensity shows concentration","Line positions show concentration; line intensity shows flame temperature","Line positions show which ion; line intensity shows the charge on the ion"],"a":1,"x":"The positions of the lines are like a fingerprint for the ion. How intense the lines are depends on the concentration. Students often swap these two ideas.","w":["The two roles are swapped. Position identifies the ion and intensity shows concentration.","","Position does not show concentration, and intensity is not used to find the flame temperature.","Intensity is linked to concentration, not to the charge on the ion."]}
{"id":"gch-255","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.1","lv":1,"q":"Which description of dry air today is closest to correct?","o":["About 80% oxygen and 20% nitrogen","About 50% nitrogen and 50% oxygen","About 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen","About 80% nitrogen and 20% carbon dioxide"],"a":2,"x":"Air is about four-fifths nitrogen and one-fifth oxygen. Carbon dioxide is only about 0.04%. A common trap is swapping the two main gases.","w":["This swaps the two gases. Nitrogen is the larger part of air, not oxygen.","Air is not an equal mix. Nitrogen makes up far more than oxygen.","","Carbon dioxide is a trace gas, only about 0.04%. The 20% is oxygen."]}
{"id":"gch-256","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.1","lv":2,"q":"Put these in the order scientists think they happened, earliest first. 1 Algae release oxygen. 2 Water vapour condenses to form oceans. 3 Volcanoes release gases. 4 Animals evolve.","o":["3, 2, 1, 4","3, 1, 2, 4","2, 3, 1, 4","3, 2, 4, 1"],"a":0,"x":"Volcanoes gave out gases first. Water vapour then condensed into oceans, where algae lived and made oxygen. Animals came later, once oxygen was available. The trap is putting animals before oxygen.","w":["","Algae live in water, so oceans had to form before algae could release oxygen.","Gases from volcanoes were the source of the water vapour, so they came first.","Animals need oxygen to breathe, so they could only evolve after algae released it."]}
{"id":"gch-257","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.1","lv":2,"q":"Over billions of years the amount of carbon dioxide in the air fell. Where did much of the carbon end up?","o":["Lost into space as the Earth cooled","Locked in sedimentary rocks and fossil fuels","Changed into nitrogen in the oceans","Stored as water vapour in the clouds"],"a":1,"x":"Carbon dioxide dissolved in the oceans and formed carbonate sediments such as limestone. Plants and algae took it in, and their remains formed fossil fuels. The carbon was locked away, not lost.","w":["Gravity holds heavy gases like carbon dioxide on Earth. Carbon did not escape into space.","","Atoms of one element do not change into another in these processes. Carbon cannot become nitrogen.","Water vapour is H₂O and contains no carbon. It is not a store for carbon."]}
{"id":"gch-258","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.1","lv":1,"q":"Scientists think the early atmosphere was like the atmospheres of Mars and Venus today. Which gas is the main one in all three?","o":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Methane","Carbon dioxide"],"a":3,"x":"The early atmosphere was mostly carbon dioxide with little or no oxygen, like Mars and Venus today. The trap is picking nitrogen, which is the main gas in Earth's air now.","w":["There was little or no oxygen in the early atmosphere. It built up later through photosynthesis.","Nitrogen is the main gas in air today, but it was not the main gas in the early atmosphere.","Methane was only present in small amounts in the early atmosphere.",""]}
{"id":"gch-259","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.1","lv":3,"q":"Dry air today is about 20% oxygen and 0.04% carbon dioxide. Roughly how many times more oxygen than carbon dioxide is in a sample of air?","o":["5 times","50 times","500 times","5000 times"],"a":2,"x":"Divide the percentages: 20 ÷ 0.04 = 500. The trap is slipping a decimal place when dividing by 0.04, which gives 50 or 5000.","w":["This comes from dividing 20 by 4 and ignoring the 0.0 in 0.04.","This comes from dividing 20 by 0.4, which has the decimal in the wrong place.","","This comes from dividing 20 by 0.004, which has one zero too many."]}
{"id":"gch-260","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which human activity adds methane to the atmosphere?","o":["Burning petrol in car engines","Growing rice in flooded paddy fields","Cutting down forests for farmland","Making electricity with wind turbines"],"a":1,"x":"Rice paddies, cattle farming and landfill sites release methane. Burning fuels mainly releases carbon dioxide, so do not mix up the two greenhouse gases.","w":["Burning petrol mainly releases carbon dioxide, not methane. This mixes up the two greenhouse gases.","","Deforestation means less CO₂ is removed from the air. It does not directly release methane.","Wind turbines release no greenhouse gases while running, so they add no methane."]}
{"id":"gch-261","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is meant by the carbon footprint of a product?","o":["The CO₂ released only while the product is being used","The total mass of carbon atoms inside the product","The CO₂ taken in by trees to make up for the product","All greenhouse gases released over its whole life cycle"],"a":3,"x":"A carbon footprint counts CO₂ and other greenhouse gases, such as methane, from making, using and disposing of a product. The trap is counting only the use stage.","w":["This counts only the use stage. A carbon footprint covers the whole life cycle, including making and disposal.","A footprint is about gases released, not the carbon atoms in the product itself.","Footprint measures emissions, not the CO₂ absorbed by trees. Offsetting is a different idea.",""]}
{"id":"gch-262","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why does large-scale deforestation increase the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere?","o":["Fewer trees take in CO₂, and burning or rotting wood releases it","Trees give out CO₂ in photosynthesis, so cutting them adds more","Cut trees turn into methane, which then changes into CO₂","Without trees, infrared cannot escape, so CO₂ builds up"],"a":0,"x":"Trees take in CO₂ for photosynthesis. Removing them means less CO₂ is absorbed. Burning or decay of the wood also releases CO₂. The trap is reversing photosynthesis.","w":["","Photosynthesis takes in CO₂ and gives out oxygen. This reverses the process.","Trees do not simply turn into methane when cut. The extra CO₂ comes from less uptake and from burning or decay.","Trees do not control infrared escape, and radiation does not make CO₂ build up. This confuses two ideas."]}
{"id":"gch-263","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Scientists predict that global warming will raise sea levels. Which statement gives a correct reason?","o":["Greenhouse gases dissolve in the sea and add to its volume","Warmer air makes rivers flow faster into the sea","Ice on land melts and the warmer ocean water expands","More rain falls only over the sea, so the water level rises"],"a":2,"x":"Melting ice sheets and glaciers on land add water to the oceans, and warmer water expands. Both raise sea level, causing coastal flooding and erosion.","w":["Dissolved gases do not add noticeable volume. The real causes are melting land ice and thermal expansion.","Faster rivers do not add extra water. The extra water comes from melting land ice.","","Rainfall patterns may change, but this is not the main reason. Melting ice and expansion are the key causes."]}
{"id":"gch-264","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A newspaper says one very hot summer proves that climate change is happening. Why is this weak scientific evidence?","o":["Summers cannot be affected by climate change","One season shows weather, not a long-term pattern in peer-reviewed data","Peer-reviewed data is always wrong when it is long-term","Temperature cannot be measured accurately in summer"],"a":1,"x":"Climate change is shown by long-term trends in many data sets, checked by peer review. One hot summer is weather. The trap is treating a single event as proof.","w":["Climate change can affect summers. The problem is that one summer is too little data.","","Long-term data is more reliable, not less. Peer review is meant to check the evidence.","Temperature can be measured accurately in any season. The weakness is the short time period."]}
{"id":"gch-265","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.3","lv":1,"q":"Some fuels burn to make sulfur dioxide. Where does the sulfur come from?","o":["Nitrogen in the air reacting with oxygen","Sulfur impurities in the fuel","Carbon in the fuel burning in too little oxygen","Hydrogen in the fuel reacting with oxygen"],"a":1,"x":"Many fuels, such as coal and some oil products, contain sulfur as an impurity. When the fuel burns, the sulfur reacts with oxygen to make sulfur dioxide. The trap is thinking the air supplies it.","w":["Nitrogen from the air makes oxides of nitrogen, not sulfur dioxide, because there is no sulfur in it.","","Burning carbon in too little oxygen makes carbon monoxide or soot, not sulfur dioxide.","Hydrogen burning only makes water, so it cannot produce a sulfur compound."]}
{"id":"gch-266","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.3","lv":1,"q":"Which two pollutants from burning fuels mainly cause acid rain?","o":["Carbon monoxide and soot","Carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide","Methane and soot","Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen"],"a":3,"x":"Sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen dissolve in rain water to make acidic solutions. Carbon monoxide and soot do not cause acid rain. The trap is choosing carbon gases because they come from burning.","w":["Carbon monoxide is toxic and soot is carbon particles, but neither makes rain acidic.","Carbon monoxide is poisonous and CO₂ mainly affects climate, so neither is the main cause of acid rain.","Methane is a fuel and soot causes global dimming, so neither is the main cause of acid rain.",""]}
{"id":"gch-267","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.3","lv":2,"q":"Diesel engines can release tiny carbon particles (soot). Which effect is linked to these particles?","o":["Global dimming and lung problems","Acid rain that damages buildings","Blood unable to carry oxygen","Oxides of nitrogen forming in the air"],"a":0,"x":"Carbon particles reflect and absorb sunlight, causing global dimming. They also get into the lungs and cause breathing problems. Acid rain is linked to sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen, not soot.","w":["","Acid rain comes from sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen, whereas soot is solid carbon and is not acidic.","This is the effect of carbon monoxide, not soot, so it mixes up two products of incomplete combustion.","Oxides of nitrogen are separate gases made at high temperatures, not an effect of soot."]}
{"id":"gch-268","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.3","lv":2,"q":"Methane burns in a limited supply of oxygen and makes carbon monoxide and water. Which equation is balanced?","o":["CH₄ + O₂ → CO + 2H₂O","2CH₄ + 4O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O","2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O","CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO + 2H₂O"],"a":2,"x":"Count oxygen atoms. 2CH₄ + 3O₂ has 6 O on the left. 2CO + 4H₂O has 2 + 4 = 6 O on the right. CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO + 2H₂O is the complete combustion pattern, which makes CO₂, not CO.","w":["Left has 2 O atoms but right has 1 + 2 = 3 O atoms, so oxygen is not balanced.","Left has 8 O atoms but right has 2 + 4 = 6 O atoms, so oxygen is not balanced.","","Left has 4 O atoms but right has 1 + 2 = 3 O atoms, so oxygen is not balanced."]}
{"id":"gch-269","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.3","lv":3,"q":"Which row correctly matches a pollutant with how it forms and one effect?","o":["Sulfur dioxide | incomplete combustion | stops blood carrying oxygen","Oxides of nitrogen | air reacting in hot engines | acid rain, breathing problems","Soot | sulfur impurities in fuel | acid rain","Carbon monoxide | air reacting in hot engines | global dimming"],"a":1,"x":"At the high temperatures in engines, nitrogen and oxygen from the air react to make oxides of nitrogen. These cause acid rain and breathing problems. The other rows each mix up a source or an effect.","w":["Sulfur dioxide comes from sulfur impurities and causes acid rain; it is carbon monoxide that stops blood carrying oxygen.","","Soot forms in incomplete combustion and causes global dimming; sulfur impurities make sulfur dioxide, not soot.","Carbon monoxide forms in incomplete combustion and is toxic; it is not made from air in engines or a cause of dimming."]}
{"id":"gch-270","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement best describes sustainable development?","o":["Using resources as quickly as possible to give people the best life now","Meeting the needs of people today without stopping future generations meeting their needs","Stopping all use of Earth's natural resources so that none are used up","Using only renewable resources and never using any finite resource"],"a":1,"x":"Sustainable development meets present needs without harming the ability of future generations to meet theirs. It does not mean stopping all resource use, and it is not just a ban on finite resources.","w":["This puts only today's needs first, so resources run out for future generations.","","Sustainable development still uses resources. It means using them carefully, not stopping use completely.","This is not the definition. Sustainable development is about the needs of future generations, not a ban on finite resources."]}
{"id":"gch-271","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Coal is a finite resource. What is the main reason?","o":["It can only be burned once","It is only found underground","It was never made from living things","It is used much faster than it forms"],"a":3,"x":"A resource is finite when it is used up faster than it forms. Coal took millions of years to form from plants but is burned in a much shorter time. Being underground or burnable is not the reason.","w":["Many fuels burn only once, including renewable ones such as wood. This is not what makes a resource finite.","Where a resource is found does not decide if it is finite. The rate of use compared with formation does.","Coal did form from living things (ancient plants). Its origin is not why it is finite.",""]}
{"id":"gch-272","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Chemists help sustainable development. Which of these is an example of this?","o":["Designing a process that uses less energy and makes less waste","Making a cheaper product that uses more crude oil each time","Finding a way to burn fuels faster to release more energy","Replacing a recyclable material with one that is thrown away"],"a":0,"x":"Sustainable development means meeting today's needs while leaving resources for the future. Using less energy and making less waste saves resources. The other options use up more resources, so they are the opposite.","w":["","Cheaper does not mean sustainable. Using more crude oil, a finite resource, uses it up faster.","Burning fuels faster uses up finite resources more quickly, which harms future generations.","Throwing away a material instead of recycling it wastes resources, so it is less sustainable."]}
{"id":"gch-273","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A shirt is made of cotton grown on a farm. A pair of trousers is made of polyester, which is made from crude oil. Which statement is correct?","o":["Both come from finite resources because they are manufactured","Cotton is finite because it comes from the ground","Cotton comes from a renewable resource and polyester from a finite one","Polyester is renewable because factories can keep making it"],"a":2,"x":"Cotton is a crop that can be regrown, so it is renewable. Polyester is made from crude oil, which is finite. A factory making a product does not make its raw material renewable.","w":["Manufacture does not decide this. Cotton is grown as a crop, so it is renewable.","Cotton is grown in soil by agriculture and can be regrown. It is not dug up like a mineral.","","Factories can keep making polyester only while crude oil lasts. The raw material is finite, so polyester is not renewable."]}
{"id":"gch-274","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A forest has 2000 mature trees. Each year 300 are cut down and 200 new trees reach maturity. How long until no mature trees are left, and is this sustainable?","o":["It never runs out, because wood is a renewable resource","20 years; not sustainable, as trees are used faster than they are replaced","About 7 years; not sustainable, because only the 300 cut matter","20 years; sustainable, because the trees grow back each year"],"a":1,"x":"The forest loses 300 − 200 = 100 trees a year, so 2000 ÷ 100 = 20 years. Wood is renewable, but use faster than replacement is not sustainable.","w":["A renewable resource only lasts if it is replaced as fast as it is used. Here it is not.","","This ignores the 200 new trees each year. The net loss is 100 a year, not 300.","The 20 years is correct, but it is not sustainable. Replacement is slower than use, so the forest runs out."]}
{"id":"gch-275","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.2","lv":1,"q":"At a sewage works, solid sewage sludge settles out of the waste water. How is the sludge then treated?","o":["It is filtered through sand and gravel beds","It is broken down by microorganisms in anaerobic digestion","It is sterilised with chlorine and put back in rivers","It is distilled to separate out the water"],"a":1,"x":"Sludge is digested by microorganisms without air (anaerobic digestion). The effluent is treated separately with aerobic biological treatment. The trap is mixing up the two parts of the process.","w":["Sand and gravel filter beds are used for drinking water, not for sewage sludge.","","Chlorine is not used on sludge, and sludge is not put into rivers. This mixes up drinking water treatment.","Distillation is too expensive for sewage. It is not how sludge is treated."]}
{"id":"gch-276","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What does the term potable water mean?","o":["Water that is safe to drink, even if it has some dissolved substances","Water that contains only H₂O molecules","Water that has been distilled","Water that has a pH of exactly 7"],"a":0,"x":"Potable water is safe to drink. It is not the same as pure water, because it usually contains dissolved substances. The trap is thinking potable means pure.","w":["","Pure water has only H₂O, but drinking water usually contains dissolved salts. This confuses potable with pure.","Distillation is one way to get water, but potable water does not have to be distilled.","Safe drinking water has a pH in a range, about 6.5 to 8.5, not exactly 7."]}
{"id":"gch-277","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A student evaporates 25 cm³ of sea water to dryness. The empty dish is 32.40 g. The dish with dry solid is 33.15 g. What mass of dissolved solid is in 100 cm³?","o":["0.75 g","0.19 g","3.00 g","30.0 g"],"a":2,"x":"Mass of solid = 33.15 − 32.40 = 0.75 g in 25 cm³. 100 cm³ is 4 times bigger, so 0.75 × 4 = 3.00 g. The trap is forgetting to scale up.","w":["This is the mass in only 25 cm³. It forgets to scale up to 100 cm³.","This divides by 4 instead of multiplying by 4, so the scaling is the wrong way round.","","This has a decimal place slip. 0.75 × 4 is 3.00, not 30.0."]}
{"id":"gch-278","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about getting drinking water from sea water is correct?","o":["Reverse osmosis uses a sand and gravel bed to hold back salt","Distillation needs very little energy because water boils easily","Chlorine is added to sea water to remove the salt","Reverse osmosis pushes water through a membrane that holds back salt ions"],"a":3,"x":"In reverse osmosis, water is forced through a partially permeable membrane at high pressure and salt ions are held back. It needs energy, as does distillation. Chlorine only kills microbes.","w":["Sand and gravel beds filter out insoluble solids only. Dissolved salt passes through.","Boiling large amounts of water needs a lot of energy, so distillation is expensive.","Chlorine kills microorganisms. It does not remove dissolved salt.",""]}
{"id":"gch-279","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A student tests four water samples. Which sample is pure water?","o":["pH 7, leaves no residue when evaporated, boils at 100 °C","pH 7, clear and has no smell","Leaves no residue when evaporated, boils at 103 °C","Has no bacteria after chlorination and tastes of nothing"],"a":0,"x":"Pure water has no dissolved substances and boils at exactly 100 °C. A neutral pH and a clear look do not prove purity. A higher boiling point shows impurities are present.","w":["","Many clear, neutral samples contain dissolved salts. This mistake treats appearance and pH as proof of purity.","A boiling point above 100 °C means something is dissolved in it, so it is not pure.","Safe or tasteless water can still contain dissolved substances. Killing bacteria does not make it pure."]}
{"id":"gch-280","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A farmer wants to get copper from soil that contains very little copper, using phytomining. Which list gives the correct order of steps?","o":["Grow plants; collect leachate from them; electrolyse the leachate","Burn the soil; grow plants in the ash; harvest them for copper metal","Grow plants; harvest and burn them; extract copper from compounds in the ash","Grow plants; harvest and crush them; filter copper metal from the juice"],"a":2,"x":"Plants absorb copper compounds from the soil. They are harvested and burned. The ash contains copper compounds, and copper is then extracted by electrolysis or displacement. Plants do not make copper metal, and a leachate comes from bioleaching.","w":["A leachate is made by bacteria in bioleaching, not by plants, so this mixes up the two methods.","Plants are grown in the soil and then burned; they absorb copper compounds, not copper metal.","","Plants take in copper compounds, not copper metal, so filtering metal from plant juice is wrong; the plants must be burned first."]}
{"id":"gch-281","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.4","lv":3,"q":"Bioleaching of a low-grade copper ore gives a leachate containing copper ions, Cu²⁺. Scrap iron is added and copper forms. Which statement explains this?","o":["Iron is more reactive, so Fe atoms lose electrons to Cu²⁺ ions, forming copper","Iron is less reactive, so it pushes copper ions out of solution as copper","Iron is a catalyst that lets the bacteria turn Cu²⁺ ions into copper metal","Bacteria oxidise the iron, which releases solid copper from the ore"],"a":0,"x":"Leachate contains copper compounds, not metal. Iron is more reactive, so it displaces copper: Fe + Cu²⁺ → Fe²⁺ + Cu. Iron loses electrons and copper ions gain them. A less reactive metal cannot displace a more reactive one.","w":["","A less reactive metal cannot displace a more reactive one; this reverses the reactivity series, as iron is more reactive than copper.","Iron is used up in the reaction, so it is a reactant, not a catalyst; bacteria are not needed at this step.","The bacteria made the leachate earlier; the copper here comes from displacement by iron, not bacteria oxidising iron."]}
{"id":"gch-282","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.4","lv":3,"q":"A council compares phytomining and bioleaching with traditional mining of copper. Which statement is correct for both new methods?","o":["They are faster than traditional mining, so copper is supplied sooner","They give pure copper metal directly, so no further step is needed","They need no land, so they cause no environmental damage at all","They can use low-grade ores and need less digging, but they are slow"],"a":3,"x":"Both methods suit low-grade ores and avoid large-scale digging and crushing of ore, but they are slow. Both give copper compounds in ash or leachate, so copper still has to be extracted, for example by electrolysis.","w":["Both methods are slower than traditional mining because plants must grow and bacteria work gradually; speed is not an advantage.","Both give copper compounds in ash or leachate, so extra steps like electrolysis or displacement are still needed.","Phytomining needs land to grow plants, and both can cause some damage; saying no damage at all is an exaggeration.",""]}
{"id":"gch-283","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.2","lv":1,"q":"A firm does a life cycle assessment (LCA) for a plastic bottle. Which of these is part of the disposal stage?","o":["Drilling for the crude oil","Moulding the plastic into bottles","Driving used bottles to a landfill site","Filling the bottles with drink"],"a":2,"x":"Disposal covers what happens at the end of the product's life, including transport to landfill. Getting crude oil is the raw materials stage. Moulding and filling are manufacturing and packaging.","w":["Getting crude oil is the raw materials stage, not disposal; this confuses the start of the life cycle with the end.","Moulding is part of manufacturing, not disposal; this confuses making the product with getting rid of it.","","Filling bottles is part of manufacturing and packaging, not disposal; this confuses packaging with end of life."]}
{"id":"gch-284","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.2","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is recycling of glass bottles, rather than reusing them?","o":["Washing them and filling them again","Using them as storage jars at home","Using them as vases for flowers","Crushing and melting them to make new glass items"],"a":3,"x":"Recycling means the glass is melted and made into new items. Reusing means the bottle keeps its shape and is used again. Refilling, storage jars and vases are all reuse, a common mix-up.","w":["Washing and refilling keeps the bottle as it is, so it is reuse, not recycling.","Using a bottle as a jar needs no melting, so it is reuse; the two terms get mixed up.","Using a bottle as a vase keeps its shape, so it is reuse, not recycling.",""]}
{"id":"gch-285","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.2","lv":2,"q":"Two groups do an LCA of the same product and get different answers about pollution. What is the best reason for this?","o":["Giving pollutants a number involves value judgements","Energy use in making a product cannot be measured","Pollutants have no effect on the environment","Only the cost of materials can be included in an LCA"],"a":0,"x":"Water, energy and resource use can be measured. Giving a number to the effect of a pollutant is a value judgement, so it is not fully objective. Different people may choose different values.","w":["","Energy use can be measured; the problem is judging pollutant effects, so this is a wrong claim about what is measurable.","Pollutants do harm the environment, so this is false; the disagreement is about how to value that harm.","LCAs include resources, energy and pollution, not only cost; this confuses an LCA with a cost estimate."]}
{"id":"gch-286","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.2","lv":3,"q":"An advert says a drinks carton is 'greener' because it causes no pollution when used. Why might this give a biased picture of its life cycle?","o":["It shows that cartons cannot be recycled","It looks only at the use stage and ignores the other stages","It counts the disposal stage twice","It includes every stage, so it must be fair"],"a":1,"x":"A full LCA covers raw materials, manufacture and packaging, use, and disposal. Showing only the use stage is a selective LCA and can mislead. Pollution in making or disposing of the carton is left out.","w":["The advert says nothing about recycling; this invents a claim that is not in the question.","","Nothing suggests disposal is counted twice; the problem is that stages are missing, not repeated.","The claim covers only the use stage, so it is not a full LCA; assuming it covers everything is the slip."]}
{"id":"gch-287","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.2","lv":2,"q":"Making 1 tonne of aluminium from ore needs 200 GJ of energy. Recycling 1 tonne needs 10 GJ. How much energy is saved by recycling 30 tonnes instead of using ore?","o":["300 GJ","190 GJ","5700 GJ","6000 GJ"],"a":2,"x":"Saving per tonne = 200 − 10 = 190 GJ. For 30 tonnes: 190 × 30 = 5700 GJ. Trap: using only one energy figure, or forgetting to multiply by 30.","w":["This is 10 × 30, the energy used to recycle, not the energy saved.","This is the saving for 1 tonne only; it forgets to multiply by 30 tonnes.","","This is 200 × 30, the energy to extract from ore; it forgets to subtract the energy used in recycling."]}
{"id":"gch-288","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.3","lv":1,"q":"Which raw materials are heated together to make soda-lime glass?","o":["Wet clay, shaped and then heated in a furnace","Sand, sodium carbonate and limestone","Sand and boron trioxide only","Crude oil and sand, heated together"],"a":1,"x":"Soda-lime glass is made by heating sand, sodium carbonate and limestone. Boron trioxide is used for borosilicate glass. Clay is used for ceramics such as pottery and bricks.","w":["This is how clay ceramics such as pottery and bricks are made, not glass.","","Sand with boron trioxide makes borosilicate glass, not soda-lime glass.","Crude oil is the source of monomers for polymers, not a raw material for glass."]}
{"id":"gch-289","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.3","lv":1,"q":"A kettle handle is made from a polymer that does not soften when heated. Why does it not soften?","o":["Its polymer chains have no bonds or forces between them","Its chains are held by weak forces that need a lot of energy to break","Its chains are very short and tightly coiled together","Its chains are joined to each other by strong cross-links"],"a":3,"x":"This is a thermosetting polymer. Strong cross-links join its chains, so it does not soften or melt when heated. Thermosoftening polymers have only weak forces between chains.","w":["Chains with no forces between them would melt very easily. Thermosetting polymers have strong links.","Weak forces between chains are found in thermosoftening polymers, which do soften when heated.","Chain length is not the reason. It is the cross-links between chains that stop softening.",""]}
{"id":"gch-290","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.3","lv":2,"q":"Pure gold is 24 carat. A ring of mass 8.0 g is made from 18 carat gold. What mass of gold is in the ring?","o":["1.4 g","2.0 g","6.0 g","10.7 g"],"a":2,"x":"18 carat means 18 parts in 24 are gold. Mass of gold = 8.0 × 18/24 = 6.0 g. The trap is using 18 out of 100 or turning the fraction upside down.","w":["This uses 18 out of 100 instead of 18 out of 24.","This is the mass of the other metals (6 parts in 24), not the gold.","","This turns the fraction upside down (24/18). It is also more than the whole ring."]}
{"id":"gch-291","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.3","lv":2,"q":"Blocks of magnesium are bolted to the steel hull of a ship. This stops the steel from rusting, even where the hull is scratched. Why?","o":["Magnesium is more reactive than iron, so it reacts instead of the iron","Magnesium forms a waterproof layer that covers the whole hull","Magnesium is less reactive than iron, so it protects the iron","Magnesium is stronger than iron, so the hull cannot crack"],"a":0,"x":"This is sacrificial protection. Magnesium is more reactive than iron, so it reacts with oxygen and water first and the iron is protected. The magnesium blocks are used up and replaced.","w":["","The blocks do not cover the hull. Covering with a layer is barrier protection, a different method.","A less reactive metal would not be sacrificed. Magnesium must be more reactive than iron.","Strength has nothing to do with rusting, which is a reaction with oxygen and water."]}
{"id":"gch-292","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.3","lv":3,"q":"One steel can is coated with tin and another with zinc. Both coatings are scratched so the steel shows. Which statement is correct?","o":["Both cans rust equally, as both coatings are metals","Only the zinc-coated can is protected, because zinc is more reactive than iron","Only the tin-coated can is protected, because tin stays on as a barrier","Neither can rusts, because the coating still covers most of the steel"],"a":1,"x":"Zinc is more reactive than iron, so it still protects the steel when scratched (sacrificial protection). Tin is less reactive, so it only works as a barrier. Once scratched, the iron rusts.","w":["The metals differ in reactivity. Only a metal more reactive than iron can protect it once scratched.","","Tin is only a barrier. Once scratched, air and water reach the iron, which then rusts.","Rusting still happens at the exposed steel. Covering most of the surface is not enough."]}
{"id":"gch-293","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.4","lv":1,"q":"In the Haber process, where does the hydrogen come from?","o":["Fractional distillation of liquid air","Natural gas","Thermal decomposition of limestone","Breaking down ammonia from the reactor"],"a":1,"x":"The hydrogen for the Haber process comes from natural gas, which is mainly methane. The nitrogen comes from the air. The trap is mixing up the sources of the two reactants.","w":["Liquid air is the source of nitrogen, not hydrogen. This mixes up the two reactants.","","Limestone breaks down into calcium oxide and carbon dioxide. It contains no hydrogen.","Ammonia is the product. Breaking it down to get hydrogen would undo the whole process."]}
{"id":"gch-294","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.4","lv":1,"q":"The gases leaving the Haber process reactor include ammonia and unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen. What happens to the unreacted gases?","o":["They are released into the air","They are burned to heat the reactor","They are dissolved in water to make fertiliser","They are recycled back into the reactor"],"a":3,"x":"The ammonia is cooled and condensed to a liquid and removed. The unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen are recycled back into the reactor. This saves raw materials because the reaction is reversible.","w":["Releasing them would waste reactants. They are recycled instead.","They are not used as a fuel in the process. This is a guess about waste gases.","Nitrogen and hydrogen are not dissolved to make fertiliser. This confuses the gases with the ammonia product.",""]}
{"id":"gch-295","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.4","lv":2,"q":"N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. A higher pressure gives a higher equilibrium yield of ammonia. What is the reason?","o":["There are fewer gas molecules on the product side than the reactant side","There are more gas molecules on the product side than the reactant side","Higher pressure lowers the activation energy of the reaction","Higher pressure makes the forward reaction less exothermic"],"a":0,"x":"There are 4 gas molecules on the left (1 + 3) and 2 on the right. Higher pressure moves the equilibrium to the side with fewer gas molecules, so more ammonia forms.","w":["","Counting the molecules wrongly. The product side has 2, the reactant side has 4.","Lowering activation energy is what a catalyst does. Pressure changes the equilibrium position, not activation energy.","Pressure does not change the energy released by the reaction. It changes the equilibrium position."]}
{"id":"gch-296","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.4","lv":2,"q":"Ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃, is used as a fertiliser. Ar: H = 1, N = 14, O = 16. What is the percentage of nitrogen by mass in it?","o":["17.5%","28%","35%","40%"],"a":2,"x":"Mr = 14 + (4 × 1) + 14 + (3 × 16) = 80. There are two N atoms, so 28 ÷ 80 × 100 = 35%. The trap is counting only one nitrogen atom.","w":["This counts only one nitrogen atom (14 ÷ 80), but there are two.","This is the mass of nitrogen (28), not a percentage. It was not divided by 80.","","This adds the mass of the hydrogen atoms to the nitrogen (32 ÷ 80). Only nitrogen should be counted."]}
{"id":"gch-297","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.4","lv":3,"q":"A company makes fertiliser from phosphate rock. It wants a product with nitrogen and phosphorus, and no sulfate. Which acid does it add to the rock?","o":["Sulfuric acid, which gives calcium phosphate and calcium sulfate","Nitric acid, which gives phosphoric acid and calcium nitrate","Phosphoric acid, which gives only calcium phosphate","Water, which dissolves the rock to give calcium phosphate"],"a":1,"x":"Phosphate rock is insoluble, so acid is needed. Nitric acid gives phosphoric acid and calcium nitrate, so there is nitrogen and phosphorus but no sulfate. Sulfuric acid adds sulfate. Phosphoric acid adds no nitrogen.","w":["Sulfuric acid makes calcium sulfate, so this product contains sulfate. The company does not want that.","","Phosphoric acid gives calcium phosphate only, so there is no nitrogen in the product.","Phosphate rock is insoluble in water. Plants cannot use it until it is treated with acid."]}
{"id":"gch-fig-004","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows two experiments with the same mass of marble and the same acid. One used powder and one used lumps. Which curve is the powder, and when did its gas production stop?","o":["B, at 80 s","A, at 120 s","A, at 40 s","B, at 40 s"],"a":2,"x":"Powder has a larger surface area, so more collisions happen each second and the reaction is faster. Curve A is steeper. It levels off at 60 cm³ when the time is 40 s.","w":["Picks the slower curve as the powder. Smaller pieces react faster, so the steeper curve is the powder.","Reads the end of the time axis, not the point where the curve first goes flat.","","Picks the slower curve as the powder, then reads the time from the other curve."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,120],"y":[0,70],"xstep":20,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"volume of gas / cm³","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,30],[20,45],[30,54],[40,60],[120,60]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[20,30],[40,45],[60,54],[80,60],[120,60]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gch-fig-005","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Hydrogen peroxide breaks down to give oxygen. Use the graph to find the mean rate of the catalysed reaction over the first 20 seconds.","o":["0.5 cm³/s","2.0 cm³/s","1.0 cm³/s","40 cm³/s"],"a":1,"x":"Read the catalyst curve at 20 s: 40 cm³ of oxygen. Mean rate = volume ÷ time = 40 ÷ 20 = 2.0 cm³/s. The catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy, so the rate is higher.","w":["Divides time by volume (20 ÷ 40), which turns the rate upside down.","Reads the 'no catalyst' curve, which is 20 cm³ at 20 s, instead of the catalyst curve.","","Gives the volume only and forgets to divide by the time."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,100],"y":[0,70],"xstep":20,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"volume of oxygen / cm³","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,26],[20,40],[40,54],[60,60],[100,60]],"label":"catalyst","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[20,20],[40,34],[60,46],[80,54],[100,60]],"label":"no catalyst","dash":true,"c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gch-fig-001","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the mass of a reactant left as a reaction goes on. What is the mean rate of reaction over the first 40 s, in terms of reactant used up?","o":["0.50 g/s","1.0 g/s","1.5 g/s","0.67 g/s"],"a":1,"x":"At 0 s the mass is 60 g. At 40 s it is 20 g. Mass used up = 60 − 20 = 40 g. Mean rate = 40 ÷ 40 = 1.0 g/s.","w":["Used the mass left at 40 s instead of the mass used up.","","Used the starting mass of 60 g instead of the change in mass, then divided by 40 s.","Divided the mass used up by the starting mass of 60 g instead of by the time."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,80],"y":[0,70],"xstep":10,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"mass of reactant / g","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,60.0],[0.5,58.536585],[1.0,57.142857],[1.5,55.813953],[2.0,54.545455],[2.5,53.333333],[3.0,52.173913],[3.5,51.06383],[4.0,50.0],[4.5,48.979592],[5.0,48.0],[5.5,47.058824],[6.0,46.153846],[6.5,45.283019],[7.0,44.444444],[7.5,43.636364],[8.0,42.857143],[8.5,42.105263],[9.0,41.37931],[9.5,40.677966],[10.0,40.0],[10.5,39.344262],[11.0,38.709677],[11.5,38.095238],[12.0,37.5],[12.5,36.923077],[13.0,36.363636],[13.5,35.820896],[14.0,35.294118],[14.5,34.782609],[15.0,34.285714],[15.5,33.802817],[16.0,33.333333],[16.5,32.876712],[17.0,32.432432],[17.5,32.0],[18.0,31.578947],[18.5,31.168831],[19.0,30.769231],[19.5,30.379747],[20.0,30.0],[20.5,29.62963],[21.0,29.268293],[21.5,28.915663],[22.0,28.571429],[22.5,28.235294],[23.0,27.906977],[23.5,27.586207],[24.0,27.272727],[24.5,26.966292],[25.0,26.666667],[25.5,26.373626],[26.0,26.086957],[26.5,25.806452],[27.0,25.531915],[27.5,25.263158],[28.0,25.0],[28.5,24.742268],[29.0,24.489796],[29.5,24.242424],[30.0,24.0],[30.5,23.762376],[31.0,23.529412],[31.5,23.300971],[32.0,23.076923],[32.5,22.857143],[33.0,22.641509],[33.5,22.429907],[34.0,22.222222],[34.5,22.018349],[35.0,21.818182],[35.5,21.621622],[36.0,21.428571],[36.5,21.238938],[37.0,21.052632],[37.5,20.869565],[38.0,20.689655],[38.5,20.