{"id":"gch-fig-003","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Curves A and B show gas made in the same reaction under two conditions. The same total volume of gas is made. What is the mean rate of reaction for curve B over the time it takes to finish?","o":["1.6 cm³/s","0.60 cm³/s","0.96 cm³/s","3.2 cm³/s"],"a":0,"x":"Curve B levels off at 30 s, when the reaction has finished. It has made 48 cm³ of gas. Mean rate = 48 ÷ 30 = 1.6 cm³/s.","w":["","Divided 48 cm³ by 80 s, the end of the axis, not by the time the reaction took to finish.","Read curve A, which finishes at 50 s, instead of curve B.","Used only the first 10 s of curve B, where the line is steepest, not the whole reaction."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,80],"y":[0,64],"xstep":10,"ystep":8,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"volume of gas / cm³","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,20],[20,32],[30,40],[40,45],[50,48],[80,48]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[10,32],[20,44],[30,48],[80,48]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gch-fig-006","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Use the graph to find the activation energy of this reaction and decide whether it is exothermic or endothermic.","o":["350 kJ, endothermic","250 kJ, endothermic","150 kJ, endothermic","250 kJ, exothermic"],"a":1,"x":"Activation energy is the rise from the reactants to the peak: 350 − 100 = 250 kJ. The products are higher than the reactants, so energy is taken in. The reaction is endothermic.","w":["Reads the height of the peak, not the climb from the reactants. Activation energy is peak minus reactants.","","Takes the peak minus the products. Activation energy starts from the reactants' level.","Gets the activation energy right but thinks a high peak means exothermic. The products are higher than the reactants, so it is endothermic."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,12],"y":[0,400],"xstep":2,"ystep":50,"xlabel":"progress of reaction","ylabel":"energy / kJ","lines":[{"pts":[[0,100],[3,100],[3.5,118.5],[4,164.8],[4.5,225],[5,285.2],[5.5,331.5],[6,350],[6.5,338.9],[7,311.1],[7.5,275],[8,238.9],[8.5,211.1],[9,200],[12,200]],"c":0}],"xnums":false,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gch-fig-007","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A student mixes an acid and an alkali in a polystyrene cup at time 0 and records the temperature. Use the graph to give the biggest temperature rise and what the graph shows.","o":["32 °C rise; exothermic, as the temperature went up","12 °C rise; endothermic, as the temperature later fell","6 °C rise; exothermic, then energy escaped to the surroundings","12 °C rise; exothermic, then energy escaped to the surroundings"],"a":3,"x":"The temperature starts at 20 °C and peaks at 32 °C, so the rise is 12 °C. It went up, so the reaction is exothermic. The later fall is energy escaping to the surroundings.","w":["Reads the peak temperature as the rise. The rise is the peak minus the starting temperature.","Thinks a later fall means endothermic. The rise at the start shows exothermic; the fall is energy lost to the air.","Uses the final temperature minus the start. The biggest rise is measured at the peak.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[16,34],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[1,26],[2,30],[3,32],[4,30.5],[5,29],[6,28],[7,27],[8,26]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
